astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_028,28,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 2.1 Legendre expansion of the direct part We denote the mutual distance between the planets as ∆ = /bardblr1−r2/bardbl. Its inverse can be expanded using Legendre polynomials as a2 ∆=a2 r2/summationdisplay n∈N/parenleftbiggr1 r2/parenrightbiggn Pn(cosS), (2) whereSis the angle between the position vectors r1andr2, andPndenotes the Legendre polynomial of degree n. Following the formalism of ( Laskar and Bou´ e 2010 ), this expression can be transformed into a Fourier series in the mean anomalies Miand written as a power series in the semi-major axis ratio α=a1/a2, U1=−Gm1m2 a2/summationdisplay n∈NFnαn;Fn=/summationdisplay k1,k2∈ZF(k1,k2) nEι(k1M1+k2M2),(3) where the indices kilabel the harmonics of the mean anomalies Mi. In all this work, we assume that we are in a domain Dexcluding collisions, that is |r1/r2|< ρ0<1. In this case 1 /∆ is analytical on Dand its Fourier expansion with double arguments (M1,M2)(Eq.3)isabsolutelyanduniformlyconvergentwith coeﬃcientsthatdecre ase exponentially with the order ( |k1|,|k2|). Throughout this paper, ιrepresents the imag- inary unit and E the exponential operator. In the planar case, the coeﬃcients F(k1,k2) n depend on the orbital eccentricities eiand the arguments",2.1 Legendre expansion of the direct part,2.1 Legendre expansion of the direct part,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180,False,1.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_029,29,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part For systems with low eccentricities and inclinations, the inverse of th e mutual distance between planets can be expanded around the circular planar proble m. Following the formalism of ( Laskar 1991 ;Laskar and Robutel 1995 ), it is expressed as a2 ∆=a2 r2(A+V)−1/2=a2 r2/summationdisplay k∈N(−1)k(2k)! 4k(k!)2VkA−k−1/2, (8) whereAandVare deﬁned as A= 1−2αcos(λ1−λ2)+α2, V= 2α/parenleftBig cos(λ1−λ2)−ρ αcosS/parenrightBig +α2/parenleftbigg/parenleftBigρ α/parenrightBig2 −1/parenrightbigg .(9) Here,ρ=r1/r2,λi=Mi+ωi+ Ωiare the planetary mean longitudes and Sis the angle between the heliocentric position vectors r1andr2. The term Acorresponds to the square of the mutual distance in the circular planar case and ad mits the Fourier expansion A−s=1 2/summationdisplay l∈Zb(l) s(α)Eιl(λ1−λ2), (10) whereb(l) s(α) are the classical Laplace coeﬃcients ( Laplace 1878 ). As long as 0 < α < α0<1,Ais bounded from zero and the Fourier series ( 10) converges absolutely and uniformly with Laplace coeﬃcients that decrease exponentially with t he order l. The Taylor expansion of (1+ x)−1/2aboutx= 0 converges for |x|<1 and has radius of convergence 1. The series ( 8)",2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180,False,1.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_030,30,"the Fourier series ( 10) converges absolutely and uniformly with Laplace coeﬃcients that decrease exponentially with t he order l. The Taylor expansion of (1+ x)−1/2aboutx= 0 converges for |x|<1 and has radius of convergence 1. The series ( 8) is thus convergent for |V|<|A|. We have A >(1−α)2. On the other hand, Vcollects the terms involving eccentric- ities and inclinations, starting at degree 1 in eccentricity, degree 2 in inclination, and hasαin factor. Thus, for suﬃciently small α, eccentricities and inclinations, |V|<|A| is satisﬁed and the series ( 8) is convergent. As in (Laskar 1991 ;Laskar and Robutel 1995 ), we express this expansion in terms of Poincar´ e variables, which, in the planar case, take the form Λi=βi√µiai, x i=/radicalbig Λi/radicalbigg 1−/radicalBig 1−e2 iEιωi. (11) The pairs (Λ i,λi) and (xi,−ι¯xi) are canonical conjugate momentum-coordinate variables, with the overbar denoting complex conjugation. 5 Inthesevariables,the directpartofthe perturbation U1canbewritten asaFourier seriesinthemeanlongitudes,whosecoeﬃcientsarepolynomialserie sdependingonthe remaining Poincar´ e variables ( Laskar 1990 ;Laskar 1991 ;Laskar and Robutel 1995 ), U1=−Gm1m2 a2/summationdisplay l1,l2∈ZCl1,l2Eι(l1λ1+l2λ2), Cl1,l2=/summationdisplay n∈N4Υn(α)xn1 1¯x¯n1 1xn2 2¯x¯n2 2,(12) wheren= (n1,¯n1,n2,¯n2) and the coeﬃcients",2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,140,320,False,1.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_031,31,"overbar denoting complex conjugation. 5 Inthesevariables,the directpartofthe perturbation U1canbewritten asaFourier seriesinthemeanlongitudes,whosecoeﬃcientsarepolynomialserie sdependingonthe remaining Poincar´ e variables ( Laskar 1990 ;Laskar 1991 ;Laskar and Robutel 1995 ), U1=−Gm1m2 a2/summationdisplay l1,l2∈ZCl1,l2Eι(l1λ1+l2λ2), Cl1,l2=/summationdisplay n∈N4Υn(α)xn1 1¯x¯n1 1xn2 2¯x¯n2 2,(12) wheren= (n1,¯n1,n2,¯n2) and the coeﬃcients Υ n(α) can be expressed analytically in terms of Laplace coeﬃcients ( Laskar and Robutel 1995 , and references therein). This formulation provides a systematic expansion of U1in powers of the eccentricities, with explicit expressions for its secular component C0,0at any desired degree.",2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,2.2 Laplace expansion of the direct part,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,108,83,False,True,280,363,False,1.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_032,32,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order The formalism presented at orders 0 and 1 for this mixed expansion c an be systemat- ically extended to arbitrary order. At each order k, we express a2/∆ as a sum of the ﬁrstk+1 terms of the classical Laplace expansion, with each term reform ulated in a way that is exact in both eandα. Speciﬁcally, we write a2 ∆=k/summationdisplay j=0Aj, (29) where each term Ajtakes the form Aj=a2 r2CjVjA−j−1/2, C j= (−1)j(2j)! 4jj!2. (30) To extract the secular component from each term, we follow the pr ocedure outlined for orders 0 and 1. The term A−j−1/2is expanded as a Fourier series in the angle λ1−λ2using Laplace coeﬃcients: A−s=1 2/summationdisplay l∈Zb(l) s(α)Eιl(λ1−λ2). (31) The expression Vjconsists of a ﬁnite sum of products of trigonometricterms involving the angles ( λi,ωi,νi) and powers of the ratios ri/ai. The product ( a2/r2)Vjcan thus be expressed as a seriesin the mean anomalies Mi, using Hansen coeﬃcients deﬁned in Eq. (6). This allows us to write Ajas a Fourier series in the mean longitudes",3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,3.3 Generalization to arbitrary order,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180,False,1.5
