astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_035,35,"Distribution functions for spheroids ©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000 Distribution functions for spheroids 5 Figure 4. Each black curve shows how Ω φ/Ωrvaries with cir- cularity cat a ﬁxed energy in a dark-halo like potential (upper panel) or a Plummer potential (lower panel). The red lines sh ow y=x. and lim vφ→+0∂Jr ∂vφ=−1 2lim vφ→+0∂Jφ ∂vφ(Jz< Jzcrit). (18) By considering an ergodic model we have derived relation- ships, (17) and (18), that are set by the potential alone and can be applied to models with any DF. Given that the derivatives of Jthat appear in equation (13) satisfy equations (17) and (18), it is now clear that the DF of even an anisotropic model must satisfy lim Jφ→0∂f/∂J z ∂f/∂J φ= 1 (Jz> Jzcrit). (19) and lim Jφ→0∂f/∂J r ∂f/∂J φ= 2 (Jz< Jzcrit) (20)","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000","©0000 RAS, MNRAS 000, 000–000",,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,176,135,False,True,0,135,False,2.5
astrobench_a877243f10,astrobench_a877243f10_chunk_036,36,"Distribution functions for spheroids 4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy As explained above, to generate a radially-biased model we should take g <Ωr/Ωt, while continuing to satisfy the con- straint lim L→0g= 2. A possible choice of gis gβ(Jr,L) =gH(Jr,L)exp[−βvsin(cπ/2)], (26) wheregHis given by equation (25). The factor exp[−βvsin(cπ/2)] multiplying gHcausesgto be less than gHwhenβv>0 and greater than gHwhenβv<0, and thus creates radial/tangential bias depending on the sign o f βv. The sine within the exponential ensures satisfaction of the fundamental requirement that g→gHasL→0. The choice of a sine is arbitrary: any function that vanishes wit h its argument and has a ﬁnite range might be used. The centre row of panels in Fig. 5 shows the structure of a population with this DF when βv= 1. The left panel of the centre row shows that moving βvto a positive value has only a minor impact on the population’s radial density proﬁle but the centre panel shows that it causes σR> σt. The right panel shows that the diﬀerence, 147 /130kms−1, in the dispersions at R= 10kpc under-plays the diﬀerence between the",4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,4.2 Introducing velocity anisotropy,,,,,Distribution functions for spheroids,James Binney,astro-ph.GA,2026-02-26T15:41:51Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23127v1,2602.23127,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180,False,2.5
