astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_029,29,"Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025) 6M. Baes 0 2 4 6 /u1D706/u1D45F2 T0.60.70.80.91/u1D709crit isotropic/u1D45FT=2 /u1D45FT=4 /u1D45FT=8 Figure 5.The critical truncation sharpness𝜉 crit(𝑟T,𝜆)for truncated Hern- quist models with different truncation radii and different Osipkov–Merritt parameters. All Osipkov–Merritt models with𝜉⩽𝜉 critare consistent. The dotted grey line corresponds to𝜆=0, i.e. isotropic models. more diverse orbital structures, however. Dark matter haloes tend to beisotropicinthecentralregionsandmoderatelytangentialatlarge radii (Taylor & Navarro 2001; Diemand et al. 2004; Ludlow et al. 2011; Svensmark et al. 2021); for galaxies and globular clusters theorbitalstructure showsalargerspreadin anisotropy(vandeVen etal.2006;Napolitanoetal.2014;Liepoldetal.2020;Santuccietal. 2022;Cohenetal.2021).Forthisreasonitisusefultoinvestigateto what extent our results can be generalised to more complex orbital structures. Theisotropicorbitalstructurecanbeconsideredasaspecialcaseof amoregeneralone-parameterfamilyoforbitalstructures,calledthe Osipkov–Merritt orbital structures (Osipkov 1979; Merritt 1985a). These models are characterised by a distribution function that de- pends on binding energy and angular momentum only through the combination𝑄=E−1 2𝜆𝐿2.Thevelocityanisotropyprofile,gener- ally defined as 𝛽(𝑟)=1−𝜎2 𝜃 𝜎2𝑟,(12) has a specific form for the Osipkov–Merritt models: 𝛽(𝑟)=𝜆𝑟2 1+𝜆𝑟2.(13) For𝜆=0,𝑄reduces toEand the Osipkov–Merritt orbital structure reduces to the isotropic one (𝛽=0). For𝜆 >0, the orbital struc- ture varies from isotropic","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)",,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,0,180,False,2.5
astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_030,30,"the combination𝑄=E−1 2𝜆𝐿2.Thevelocityanisotropyprofile,gener- ally defined as 𝛽(𝑟)=1−𝜎2 𝜃 𝜎2𝑟,(12) has a specific form for the Osipkov–Merritt models: 𝛽(𝑟)=𝜆𝑟2 1+𝜆𝑟2.(13) For𝜆=0,𝑄reduces toEand the Osipkov–Merritt orbital structure reduces to the isotropic one (𝛽=0). For𝜆 >0, the orbital struc- ture varies from isotropic in the centre to purely radial (𝛽→1) at verylargeradii.ThistypeofOsipkov–MerrittmodeliscalledTypeI in Merritt (1985a) and is commonly adopted in the literature (e.g. Merritt 1985b; Carollo et al. 1995; Ciotti & Lanzoni 1997; Widrow 2000; Łokas & Mamon 2001; Di Cintio et al. 2015; Di Cintio & Casetti 2020; Ciotti et al. 2019). Finally, for𝜆<0, the orbital struc- ture gradually changes from isotropic in the centre to completely tangential (𝛽→−∞) at𝑟=𝑟 a≡1/√ −𝜆. These so-called Type II Osipkov–Merritt models are only physically meaningful for spher- ical models with a finite extent with𝑟 a⩽𝑟T, or equivalently with 𝜆⩾𝜆min=−1/𝑟2 T. Owing to this limitation, tangential Osipkov– Merritt models have only been considered in a limited number of studies, such as Polyachenko & Shukhman (1974), Osipkov (1979), and the previous papers in this series (Paper I–Paper IV). The consistency conditions for any Osipkov–Merritt model de- pend","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)",,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,140,320,False,2.5
astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_031,31,"2023), we can numerically calculate𝜉 crit(𝑟T,𝜆)for each (𝑟T,𝜆)combination. The result is shown in Fig. 5 for three different values of𝑟 T. At fixed𝑟 T,𝜉critis a decreasing function of𝜆, which implies that models with more tangential orbital structures allow sharper truncations. This is a logical consequence of the fact that it is easier to populate spherical models with nearly circular than with highly eccentric orbits. In the limit𝜆𝑟2 T=−1or𝜆=𝜆 min, we find 𝜉crit=1for all𝑟 T, which means that all truncations are allowed, including the infinitely sharp truncation. An interesting observation is that smoothly truncated models allow a range of orbital struc- tures,includingradiallyanisotropicOsipkov–Merrittmodels.Thisis again a stark difference from sharply truncated models, where only the extreme tangential Osipkov–Merritt model with𝜆=−1/𝑟2 Tis consistent (Paper II). Asaconcreteexample,weconsiderthetruncatedHernquistmodel with𝑟 T=4and𝜉=0.6.Forthismodel,allOsipkov–Merrittmodels with−0.0625⩽𝜆⩽0.1828are consistent. On the bottom row of Fig. 6 we show the distribution function for the most tangential, the isotropic, and the most radial models in this range. The distribution functions are shown in turning-point space, which allows for an easier comparison than when shown as a function of𝑄, since the quantity𝑄depends explicitly on the value","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)",,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,420,600,False,2.5
astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_032,32,"Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025) Dynamical models with a finite extent V7 0 1 2 3 4 5 /u1D45Fperi012345/u1D45Fapo circular orbitsisotropic:/u1D706=0 0 1 2 3 4 5 /u1D45Fperi012345/u1D45Fapo circular orbitsunpopulated orbits radial:/u1D706=0.1828 10−410−310−210−1100 /u1D453(/u1D45Fperi,/u1D45Fapo) 0 1 2 3 4 5 /u1D45Fperi012345/u1D45Fapo circular orbitstruncated region /u1D45FT=4,/u1D709=0.6 tangential:/u1D706=−0.0625 0 1 2 3 4 5 /u1D45Fperi012345/u1D45Fapo circular orbitstruncated region unpopulated orbits /u1D45FT=4,/u1D709=0.6 isotropic:/u1D706=0 0 1 2 3 4 5 /u1D45Fperi012345/u1D45Fapo circular orbitstruncated region unpopulated orbits /u1D45FT=4,/u1D709=0.6 radial:/u1D706=0.1828 10−410−310−210−1100 /u1D453(/u1D45Fperi,/u1D45Fapo) Figure 6.Distribution functions in turning-point space,𝑓(𝑟 peri,𝑟apo), for non-truncated and truncated Hernquist models. The top row corresponds to the non-truncated Hernquist model and shows the distribution function for the isotropic model and for the radial Osipkov–Merritt model with𝜆=0.1828. The bottomrowcorrespondstothetruncatedHernquistmodelwith𝑟 T=4and𝜉=0.6.Itshowsthedistributionfunctions,fromlefttoright,forthemosttangential Osipkov–Merritt model (𝜆=𝜆 min=−0.0625), the isotropic model, and the most radial Osipkov–Merritt model (𝜆=𝜆 max=0.1828). truncatedmodels,whichallowonlytheextremetangentialOsipkov– Merritt model (Paper II).","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)","MNRAS000, 1–10 (2025)",,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,195,150,False,True,0,150,False,2.5
