astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_000,0,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 1. INTRODUCTION Constraining the Milky W ay gravitational poten- tial is central to connecting stellar kinematics to the Galaxy’s mass distribution and secular evolution ( J. Bland-Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016 ). Even in the solar neighborhood, degeneracies persist between the halo shape and the disk and bulge/bar contributions. While the dark matter halo is often approximated by a spherical NFW profile ( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ), its precise geometry remains a subject of ongoing de- bate. Many recent analyses favor a flattened, oblate configuration ( q < 1) to better match kinematic con- straints ( Y. Huang et al. 2024 ), although alternative models suggesting a prolate shape have also been pro- posed ( L. Zhu et al. 2026 ). In the disk, additional un- certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 km s−1kpc−1) and “slow” ( ∼40 km s−1kpc−1) so- lutions supported by different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999 ;J. L. Sanders et",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_001,1,"additional un- certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 km s−1kpc−1) and “slow” ( ∼40 km s−1kpc−1) so- lutions supported by different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999 ;J. L. Sanders et al. 2019 ;J. P . Clarke Corresponding author: Long W ang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cnet al. 2019a ;S. Lucchini et al. 2024 ;J. A. S. Hunt & E. V asiliev 2025 ). The morphology and dynamical nature of the spiral arms are likewise uncertain ( R. J. J. Grand et al. 2012 ;G. H. Hunter et al. 2024 ;Y. R. Khalil et al. 2025 ). Stellar tidal streams—formed by the gradual stripping of star clusters—provide phase-coherent tracers of the Galactic potential ( S. E. Koposov et al. 2010 ;S. L. J. Gibbons et al. 2014 ). Globular-cluster streams are typically narrow and prominent (e.g., M. Odenkirchen et al. 2001 ), but they often probe the potential at larger Galactocentric distances. In contrast, open- cluster streams reside in the disk and are therefore par- ticularly sensitive to perturbations from the bar, spi- ral arms,",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_002,2,"proper-motion catalogs and Hipparcos as- trometry to characterize membership within the tidal radius ( M. A. C. Perryman et al. 1998 ). S. Röser et al. (2011 ) extended the search using the PPMXL catalog, providing early evidence that the cluster extends beyond its classical tidal boundary . The extended tails became accessible with Gaia (Gaia Collaboration et al. 2016 , 2018 ;S. Meingast et al. 2019 ). In particular, S. Röser et al. (2019 ) mapped tidal-tail members in Gaia DR2 using the convergent point (CP) method, establishing an empirical reference for the stream orientation near the cluster. Numerical simulations are essential for connecting the present-day stream morphology to the underlying Galac- tic dynamics. Early N-body models primarily focused on reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo- lution of its core properties within a static, axisymmet- ric Galactic potential ( Y. O. Chumak et al. 2005 ;A. Ernst et al. 2011 ). The Gaia detection of asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com- plex than can be captured by an axisymmetric model ( T. Jerabkova",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_003,3,"potential ( Y. O. Chumak et al. 2005 ;A. Ernst et al. 2011 ). The Gaia detection of asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com- plex than can be captured by an axisymmetric model ( T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ). Recent work has therefore begun to incorporate non-axisymmetric structure; for example, G. F. Thomas et al. (2023 ) explored the influence of a rotating bar and a flattened halo. However, the joint impact of the bar and spiral arms—and, in particular, how their pattern speeds map into observable stream diagnostics—remains insuﬀiciently explored. In this paper we perform a systematic survey of bar and spiral pattern speeds using high-resolution PETAR N-body simulations in a multi-component AGAMA Milky W ay potential. W e confront the models with Gaia EDR3 using CP/CCP kinematic selection and an adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching procedure. W e emphasize a single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution in a stream-aligned coordinate frame, including a prominent density peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc . W e",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_004,4,"matching procedure. W e emphasize a single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution in a stream-aligned coordinate frame, including a prominent density peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc . W e find that models with Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1and Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1 best reproduce the observed density structure, support- ing a relatively slow Galactic bar. This paper is organized as follows. Section 2.1 de- scribes the Galactic potential model and the N-body simulation setup. Section 2.3 presents the simulated stream morphologies and kinematics across the explored (Ωb,Ωs)parameter space. In Section 3we compare the simulations to observations, using the Röser CP-selected sample and Gaia EDR3 to constrain the pattern speeds via stream orientation and density structure. W e discuss limitations and future observational tests in Section 4.2. N-BODY SIMULA TIONS 2.1. Galactic potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented using the AGAMA framework ( E. V asiliev 2019 ). Our po- tential is based on the multi-component model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 )4, which includes contri- butions from",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_005,5,"potential The Galactic gravitational potential was implemented using the AGAMA framework ( E. V asiliev 2019 ). Our po- tential is based on the multi-component model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 )4, which includes contri- butions from the supermassive black hole Sgr A∗, the nuclear star cluster, the nuclear stellar disk, the Galac- tic bar, the Galactic disk (comprising both axisymmetric and spiral arm components), and the dark matter halo. T o account for the flattened dark matter distribution observed in recent studies ( Y. Huang et al. 2024 ;K. Mal- han & R. A. Ibata 2019 ;R. Ibata et al. 2024 ), we mod- ified the original dark matter halo by replacing it with a triaxial spheroidal component. F ollowing Y. Huang et al. (2016 ), this component is parameterized as a gen- eralized NFW profile ( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ) with the following configuration: • a scale radius rs= 14.39 kpc ; • a vertical-to-radial axis ratio (flattening) q= 0.82; • a scale density ρ= 1.357×107M⊙kpc−3; • a profile shape defined by",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_006,6,"2021 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ) via galpy as an axisymmetric baseline, representing a potential de- void of both bar and spiral arm perturbations. These models are summarized in T able 1, providing a frame- work to systematically isolate the influence of each non- axisymmetric component. The simulation reference frame is anchored by a Sun- Galactic center distance of R0= 8.179 kpc and a so- lar circular velocity of Vc(R0) = 229 km s−1(GRA V- ITY Collaboration et al. 2022 ;G. H. Hunter et al. 4The original potential configuration is available at https://github.com/GalacticDynamics-Oxford/Agama/blob/ master/py/example_mw_potential_hunter24.py 3 T able 1. Summary of Galactic potential configurations. Set Ωb Ωs Set 1 (Bar only) Ωgrid — Set 2 (Bar + Fixed arms) Ωgrid 22.5 Set 3 (Fixed bar + Arms) 37.5 Ωgrid Baseline (Axisymmetric) MWPotential2014 Notes. All pattern speeds are in units of km s−1kpc−1. The symbol Ωgrid denotes the set of discrete pattern speeds: 10, 20, 25, 29, 35, 37.5, 40, 42.5, 45, 50, 55. 2024 ), ensuring consistency with local kinematic con- straints. F urthermore, the solar peculiar motion is taken",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_007,7,"km s−1kpc−1. The symbol Ωgrid denotes the set of discrete pattern speeds: 10, 20, 25, 29, 35, 37.5, 40, 42.5, 45, 50, 55. 2024 ), ensuring consistency with local kinematic con- straints. F urthermore, the solar peculiar motion is taken from R. Schönrich et al. (2010 ), with (U⊙, V⊙, W⊙) = (11.1,12.24,7.25) km s−1. 0.0 2.5 5.0 7.5 10.0 12.5 15.0 17.5 20.0 R [kpc]140160180200220Circular velocity [km/s] Hunter 24 without spiral arms Hunter 24 with spiral arms This work without spiral arms This work With spiral arms Figure 1. Circular-velocity curves: G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 ) without (blue) and with (red) spiral arms; potential model used in this study without (green) and with (cyan) spiral arms. Purple dot line represents R = 8.179kpc, which is close to the Sun’s position used in this work 2.2. Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performance N- body code PETAR (L. W ang et al. 2020 ) to simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). PETAR has the ability",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1260,1440,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_008,8,"In this work, we employed the high-performance N- body code PETAR (L. W ang et al. 2020 ) to simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). PETAR has the ability to eﬀiciently handle both the internal collisional dynamics of the star cluster and the external Galactic tidal field. Specifically , PETAR employs a hybrid algorithm that effectively accounts for gravitational interactions across all spatial scales by combining dedicated methods for short-, middle-, and long-range forces using sdar and fdps (L. W ang 2020 ; S. J. Aarseth 2003 ;J. Barnes & P . Hut 1986 ;M. Iwa- sawa et al. 2016 ,2020 ;D. Namekata et al. 2018 ). This multi-scale treatment ensures the high numerical preci- sion and computational eﬀiciency required to simulateT able 2. Present-day parameters of the Hyades (in the ICRS frame) Parameter V alue RA [deg] 67.985 Dec [deg] 17.012 Distance [pc] 47.501 pm(RA, cos(Dec)) [mas/yr] 101.005 pm(Dec) [mas/yr] −28.490 Radial velocity [km/s] 39.96 Age [Myr] 648 the long-term evolution and mass loss of stellar systems over",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1400,1580,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_009,9,"(in the ICRS frame) Parameter V alue RA [deg] 67.985 Dec [deg] 17.012 Distance [pc] 47.501 pm(RA, cos(Dec)) [mas/yr] 101.005 pm(Dec) [mas/yr] −28.490 Radial velocity [km/s] 39.96 Age [Myr] 648 the long-term evolution and mass loss of stellar systems over several hundred megayears. The present-day parameters adopted for the Hyades cluster in our simulations were partially based on L. W ang & T. Jerabkova (2021 ), as shown in T able 2. Then we used the Astropy SkyCoord package to trans- form the present-day parameters into Galactocentric po- sitions and velocities ( Astropy Collaboration et al. 2013 , 2018 ,2022 ). T o simulate the dynamical evolution of the Hyades stream, we first determined the initial phase-space coor- dinates of the cluster 648 Myr ago ( L. W ang & T. Jer- abkova 2021 ). F or each gravitational potential model, we performed a backward integration of a test particle starting from the cluster’s current Galactocentric posi- tion. However, due to internal dynamical interactions and centroid shifts inherent in N-body systems, the fi- nal centroid of the simulated cluster typically",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1540,1720,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_010,10,"model, we performed a backward integration of a test particle starting from the cluster’s current Galactocentric posi- tion. However, due to internal dynamical interactions and centroid shifts inherent in N-body systems, the fi- nal centroid of the simulated cluster typically deviates from the observed present-day position, even if a test particle returns to its origin. T o account for this dis- crepancy , we used the initial conditions derived from the test-particle integration as a first guess for a Nelder- Mead optimization ( J. A. Nelder & R. Mead 1965 ). W e iteratively refined these initial coordinates until the sim- ulated cluster’s centroid at the end of the 648 Myr evo- lution satisfied the following convergence criteria: the positional differences in xandywere less than 100 pc , thezdifference was less than 20 pc , the velocity mag- nitude deviation was below 10 km s−1, and the angular misalignment between the simulated and observed ve- locity vectors was within 5◦. W e employed the McLuster package ( A. H. W. Küp- per et al. 2011 ;L. W ang et al.",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1680,1860,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_011,11,"was below 10 km s−1, and the angular misalignment between the simulated and observed ve- locity vectors was within 5◦. W e employed the McLuster package ( A. H. W. Küp- per et al. 2011 ;L. W ang et al. 2019 ) to construct the ini- tial model of the Hyades cluster. The cluster was param- eterized as a Plummer sphere with an initial total mass of800M⊙and an initial half-mass radius of rh= 1 pc . Individual stellar masses were sampled from a P . Kroupa (2001 ) initial mass function (IMF). T o account for stellar evolution, we use the sse and bse modules ( J. R. Hurley et al. 2000 ,2002 ) updated by S. Banerjee et al. (2020 ), 4 assuming a solar metallicity of Z= 0.02 throughout the simulation. 2.3. Simulation Results 2.3.1. Stream Morphology W e first summarize the qualitative dependence of the Hyades stream morphology on (Ωb,Ωs), and we then use the orientation- and density-based observational com- parisons in Section 3to identify the subset of models consistent with Gaia . W e first explored",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1820,2000,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_012,12,"2 3 4 v,sim v,pred (RRcl)(kms1) b = 37.5km/s, s = 55km/s Figure 9. Same as Figure 8, but with the CCP method applied to remove the linear dependence in the velocity along the convergent point direction. Γis the slope of linear relationship between V||,sim−V||,pred andR−Rcl. Figure 10. Gaia sources remaining after the isochrone fil- tering. The orange points represent the theoretical isochrone used for the selection. field star contamination near the Galactic disk makesit extremely diﬀicult to identify members in the more diffuse regions of the stream. The lack of radial veloc- ity (R V) data for the majority of the candidate stars in the distant tails makes it diﬀicult to uniquely distin- guish true stream members from the background pop- ulation. F uture large-scale spectroscopic surveys like WEA VE ( G. Dalton et al. 2012 ) and 4MOST ( R. S. de Jong et al. 2019 ) will be crucial for confirming the chem- ical and kinematic signatures of the distant tidal tail members. Integrating these 6D phase-space data with chemical tagging will allow for a more robust",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_013,13,"( R. S. de Jong et al. 2019 ) will be crucial for confirming the chem- ical and kinematic signatures of the distant tidal tail members. Integrating these 6D phase-space data with chemical tagging will allow for a more robust test of the Galactic potential models presented in this work. Alter- natively , the observed asymmetry may have a physical origin. A significant past collision or a series of close encounters with GMCs could have led to the structural disruption or erasure of one side of the stream ( M. Gieles et al. 2006 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ). Beyond these observational constraints and potential stochastic events, the results of this study are also sub- ject to several inherent simplifications and uncertainties in our numerical modeling approach. In this study , we varied Ωbwhile keeping its length and strength constant. In reality , the bar’s length is often correlated with Ωb, with slower bars typically being longer ( E. Athanassoula 2003 ). F urthermore, the Galactic bar and spiral arms 12 0.10 0.05 0.000.050.10Yrot [kpc]b=37.5 km/s, s=10 km/s 0.000.020.040.060.080.100.120.14Normalized",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_014,14,"0.4 0.2 0.0 0.2 0.4 Xrot [kpc] Figure 11. In each subplot, Upper panel: Spatial distribution of the model (grey points) and the corresponding selected final members (red points). Both the model and final members are shown in the stream-aligned coordinate system (Xrot, Yrot), where thexrot -axis is aligned with the principal direction of the stream’s elongation. Lower panel: Comparison of the longitudinal density distribution along the stream for the model (grey lines) and the final members (red histogram). may undergo secular evolution, meaning their pattern speeds could be time-dependent ( T. Hilmi et al. 2020 ). By adopting a static bar geometry and Ωb, our model might slightly shift the exact resonance positions, which could influence the predicted stream morphology . In addition, the intrinsic parameters of the Hyades cluster, specifically its age and initial mass, also intro- duce uncertainties. While we adopted an age of 648 Myr and an initial mass of 800M⊙based on recent literature (L. W ang & T. Jerabkova 2021 ), different age estimates ranging from 600 to800 Myr (e.g., M. A. C. Perryman et",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_015,15,"uncertainties. While we adopted an age of 648 Myr and an initial mass of 800M⊙based on recent literature (L. W ang & T. Jerabkova 2021 ), different age estimates ranging from 600 to800 Myr (e.g., M. A. C. Perryman et al. 1998 ;S. De Gennaro et al. 2009 ;S. Reino et al. 2018 ;Gaia Collaboration et al. 2018 ) would directly af- fect the simulated length of the tidal tails. Similarly , the exact solar position ( R0) and velocity ( v⊙) are still sub-jects of refinement. Since the transformation from ICRS to Galactocentric coordinates is highly sensitive to these solar parameters, small variations in (R0, V⊙)could lead to systematic shifts in the estimated orbit of the Hyades (M. J. Reid et al. 2019 ;GRA VITY Collaboration et al. 2022 ). F urthermore, determining the precise initial phase- space coordinates of the cluster 648 Myr ago remains a challenge. Although we utilized the Nelder-Mead op- timization method to refine the backward-integrated or- bit, the chaotic nature of orbits in a non-axisymmetric potential means that the “initial guess” may not be",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_016,16,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 5. CONCLUSIONS W e modeled the formation of the Hyades tidal stream with direct N-body simulations ( PETAR ) in a multi- component Milky W ay potential implemented with AGAMA and including a rotating bar and spiral arms. By systematically varying the bar and spiral pattern speeds, we quantified how non-axisymmetric forcing re- shapes the stream orientation, length, and internal den- sity structure over the ∼648 Myr evolution of the clus- ter. W e then confronted the models with observations by combining CP/CCP-based kinematic selection on Gaia EDR3 with an adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching technique. The key observational constraint is the stream-aligned longitudinal density profile of the Gaia candidates. This profile shows a prominent peak at Yrot≈0.1 kpc , which is consistent with the overdensity reported by T. Jer- abkova et al. (2021 ). W e find that this feature is best reproduced by models with Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1and Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1, supporting a relatively slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that the in- clusion",5. CONCLUSIONS,5. CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_017,17,"T. Jer- abkova et al. (2021 ). W e find that this feature is best reproduced by models with Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1and Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1, supporting a relatively slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that the in- clusion of spiral arms is not negligible and Ωsaround this intermediate value are favored. More broadly , our results demonstrate that nearby open-cluster streams provide a complementary , local avenue to constrain non-axisymmetric Galactic dynamics using not only the global stream orientation but also its fine-grained den- sity structure.ACKNOWLEDGMENTS L W thanks the support from the National Natural Science F oundation of China through grant 12573041 and 12233013, the High-level Y outh T alent Project (Provincial Financial Allocation) through the grant 2023HYSPT0706, the F undamental Research F unds for the Central Universities, Sun Y at-sen Univer- sity (2025QNPY04). TJ acknowledges the support from the MUNI A ward in Science and Humanities",5. CONCLUSIONS,5. CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,195,150,False,True,140,290,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_018,18,"( J. F. Navarro et al. 1997 ) with the following configuration: • a scale radius rs= 14.39 kpc ; • a vertical-to-radial axis ratio (flattening) q= 0.82; • a scale density ρ= 1.357×107M⊙kpc−3; • a profile shape defined by an inner slope γ= 1 , an outer slope β= 3 , and a transition sharpness α= 1 . These parameters were specifically calibrated to en- sure that the resulting circular velocity curve of our modified potential remains highly consistent with the original model described by G. H. Hunter et al. (2024 ) as shown in Fig. 1. The dynamical impact of various resonance conditions is investigated through three distinct sets of potential configurations, generated by varying ΩbandΩs. In ad- dition to these non-axisymmetric setups, we adopted the MWPotential2014 model ( J. Bovy 2015 ;L. W ang & T. Jerabkova 2021 ;T. Jerabkova et al. 2021 ) via galpy as an axisymmetric baseline, representing a potential de- void of both bar and spiral arm perturbations. These models are summarized in T able 1, providing a frame- work to systematically isolate",1. INTRODUCTION,1. INTRODUCTION,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160,True,4.5
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_019,19,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS Our comparison of the simulated and observed stream morphology and density structure favors models with Ωb≃40–45 km s−1kpc−1and Ωs= 22.5 km s−1kpc−1; here we discuss limitations and potential systematics af- fecting this inference. A striking feature of the observed Gaia data is the pro- nounced asymmetry between the two tidal tails, with one side appearing significantly more populated than the other. In contrast, while our simulated models un- der the influence of the bar and spiral arms do exhibit some degree of asymmetry , they do not fully reproduce the near-disappearance of one tail seen in the observa- tions. A possible explanation is that the high level of 11 4 3 2 1 01234v,sim(kms1) No spiral arms, b = 37.5km/s No spiral arms, b = 40km/s No spiral arms, b = 42.5km/s No spiral arms, b = 45km/s 4 3 2 1 01234v,sim(kms1) b = 37.5km/s, s = 22.5km/s b = 40km/s, s = 22.5km/s b = 42.5km/s, s = 22.5km/s",4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,4. DISCUSSION AND LIMIT A TIONS,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,4.5
