astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_017,17,"peak halo-to-stellar mass ratio, the total stellar mass of the satellite, the stellar and DM mass fractions contributed within the solar circle (with respect to the total accreted material from subhalos with Mpeak>109M⊙), and the most common accretion redshift of stripped stars. temporal consistency (P. S. Behroozi et al. 2012b). This results in a smoother and more physically meaningful definition of the main halo center across snapshots. We use CT for two key purposes: (i) computing particle positions relative to the main halo center, and (ii) iden- tifying the accretion of diffuse DM. Specifically, a DM particle is considered accreted once it crosses within the virial radius of the CT-defined main halo. With the halo catalogs and merger trees in hand, we proceed to reconstruct the merger histories by tracking individual DM and star particles back in time. We begin by selecting all particles located within a solar neighbor- hood analog at z= 0, defined as |r−r⊙|<2 kpc and |z| ≤1.5 kpc, where r is the spherical radius and r⊙is defined as 8 kpc, as in L. Necib et al.",I II III IV I II III IV,I II III IV I II III IV,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_018,18,"by selecting all particles located within a solar neighbor- hood analog at z= 0, defined as |r−r⊙|<2 kpc and |z| ≤1.5 kpc, where r is the spherical radius and r⊙is defined as 8 kpc, as in L. Necib et al. (2019).8 For each selected particle, we trace its trajectory through earlier snapshots and determine its subhalo membership. A particle is considered bound to a sub- halo at a given snapshot if it lies within the subhalo’s virial radius R200mand its velocity falls within 2.5 σof the subhalo’s internal velocity dispersion. This criterion is applied consistently to both DM and stellar particles. To ensure robust identification, we restrict our analysis to subhalos that contain at least 10 star particles. Moving backward in time, we identify a DM particle as accreted if it remains associated with the same subhalo in at least six out of the last nine snapshots, including the current one. For star particles, we use a slightly relaxed criterion, requiring membership in three out of the last four snapshots. The difference is due to the star particles needing to",I II III IV I II III IV,I II III IV I II III IV,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_019,19,"six out of the last nine snapshots, including the current one. For star particles, we use a slightly relaxed criterion, requiring membership in three out of the last four snapshots. The difference is due to the star particles needing to be “born” out of the gas, and therefore cannot be tracked arbitrarily far. This tempo- ral coherence condition minimizes contamination from 8Ideally, this cut should be performed in cylindrical coordinates, but the discrepancy is negligible given the focus on the solar neighborhood, which includes a cut to only investigate particles close to the disk.transient flybys and in-situ particles that temporarily meet the spatial and kinematic criteria. We define the particle’s accretion redshift, zacc, as the last snapshot at which it is bound to its subhalo9. For both DM and stellar particles originating from mergers, we record the peak mass of the associated sub- halo, the redshift at which the particle was accreted (zacc), and the particle’s present-day kinematics at snap- shot 600. Table 2 lists the top four mergers that con- tributed the greatest fraction of accreted stellar particles at",I II III IV I II III IV,I II III IV I II III IV,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_020,20,"a halo identified as the main halo by Rockstar may instead appear as merging into the CT main branch. As a re- sult, the classification of mergers occurring before z∼3 may therefore contain ambiguity. There are also cases in which our algorithm misidenti- fies DM particles as belonging to a merger when they do not originate from the accreted subhalo, which we refer to as contamination. For each merger, we estimate this contamination by identifying particles that are classified as belonging to an accreted subhalo, but that were al- ready located within the host halo radius prior to the subhalo’s formal accretion. We then compute the con- tamination fraction as the ratio of these particles to the total number of particles assigned to the merger. For more massive systems ( Mpeak≈1011M⊙), contamina- tion from flybys or in situ particles within the main halo can account for up to 10% of particles identified as ac- creted. For lower-mass mergers ( Mpeak≈1010M⊙), this contamination fraction is typically reduced to 1–5%. Additionally, a single physical subhalo is occasionally fragmented into multiple subhalos in",I II III IV I II III IV,I II III IV I II III IV,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_021,21,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host halo as discrete bounded ob- jects at various redshifts. Among these, the most signif- icant contributors to the local stellar population—those that deposit a noticeable fraction of stars into the solar neighborhood—can be tracked using their stellar coun- terparts. Based on this, we further classify the resolved mergers into three subcategories: (1) the top four merg- ers that contribute the most stellar mass locally and can be reliably tracked, which we call traceable mergers and are ranked by their contribution to the stellar con- tent in the solar neighborhood, (2) young untraceable mergers that are recent and massive but lack clear stel- lar signatures, and (3) old untraceable mergers that are too ancient or phase-mixed to be associated with iden- tifiable stellar debris. An illustration of the fractional m12i m12f m12m m12w m12b m12c0.00.20.40.60.81.0FractionDiffuse Young untraceable Old untraceable Merger IMerger II Merger III Merger IVFigure 3. Fractional contributions of the individual com- ponents to the local DM population.",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_022,22,"with iden- tifiable stellar debris. An illustration of the fractional m12i m12f m12m m12w m12b m12c0.00.20.40.60.81.0FractionDiffuse Young untraceable Old untraceable Merger IMerger II Merger III Merger IVFigure 3. Fractional contributions of the individual com- ponents to the local DM population. The diffuse compo- nent is generally the dominant contributor across the m12 sample. By contrast, the DM contributions from the top mergers—ranked by their associated stellar mass in the so- lar neighborhood—vary substantially, and in some cases can be negligible relative to the other components. The exact numerical values of all fractions are listed in Table. S2. contributions of the individual components to the local DM population is shown in Fig. 3.10. In this section, we describe how mergers are treated throughout the analysis. We begin by studying trace- able mergers in Sec. 3.1. In Sec. 3.1.1, we introduce the statistical metric used in this work, and in Secs. 3.1.2 and 3.1.3 we apply it to traceable mergers to quantify stellar–DM correlations. We then extend this analysis in Sec. 3.1.4, where we develop an improved mapping from stellar kinematics to",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_023,23,"case, the stellar velocity distribution appears to closely follow that of the DM. However, to quantitatively assess the level of agree- 10The exact values of these fractions are listed in Table S2 9 400 200 0 200 400 vr [km/s]01234103f(vr)[km/s]1 m12i Merger IDark Matter Stars 0 100 200 300 400 500 vt [km/s]0246810103f(vt)[km/s]1 Mass: 6.45×1010 M zacc = 1.17 0 100 200 300 400 500 |v| [km/s] 051015103f(|v|)[km/s]1 |z|<1.5kpc |rr|<2.0kpc Figure 4. Example velocity distribution for the largest m12i major merger in radial component vr, transverse component vt and speed |⃗ v|. It can be noticed that the stellar velocity distributions roughly follow the DM counterpart. ment, we require a more rigorous statistical measure. To this end, we employ the Earth Mover’s Distance (EMD). 3.1.1. Earth Mover’s Distance The Earth Mover’s Distance, also known as the Wasserstein-1 distance, quantifies how different two dis- tributions are by the minimal work needed to move the probability mass from one to the other, where work =mass×distance (C. Villani 2003, 2009). For- mally, for probability measures P,QonRdand ground cost/metric c(x, y) =||x−y||, W1(P, Q)",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_024,24,"different two dis- tributions are by the minimal work needed to move the probability mass from one to the other, where work =mass×distance (C. Villani 2003, 2009). For- mally, for probability measures P,QonRdand ground cost/metric c(x, y) =||x−y||, W1(P, Q) = inf γ∈Π(P,Q)Z Rd×Rdc(x, y)dγ(x, y),(2) where we sum over γ∈Π(P, Q), with Π( P, Q) being the set of joint distributions on the product space whose marginals are PandQrespectively. In 1D, there are convenient closed forms: if FandGare the Cumulative Density Functions (CDFs) of PandQ,",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,112,86,False,True,560,646,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_025,25,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributor to the local DM popula- tion is the diffuse component. This component includes both DM smoothly accreted onto the host halo and un- resolved contributions from low-mass subhaloes below the resolution limit of the simulation (i.e. might not have enough star particles to get correctly traced). For theLatte suite, we define the resolution threshold for resolved subhalos as 109M⊙in total mass. This thresh- old is scaled proportionally with the DM particle mass in the lower- and higher-resolution versions of each sim- ulation, as we discuss in our resolutions tests (Sec. 6.2). The diffuse component constitutes a substantial frac- tion of the local DM population—often exceeding the contribution from resolved mergers. The exact fraction varies significantly depending on the specific accretion history of the host halo and can range from approxi- mately 40% to as high as 80% (see Tab.S2). As shown in Fig. 11, the diffuse component typically exhibits a smoothly growing profile over time, with modest jumps that coincide",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_026,26,"mental changes expected from increased parameteriza- tion—indicates that the diffuse velocity distribution is intrinsically non-Gaussian. In particular, we find that the best-fit location parameter is consistent with zero, while the remaining shape parameters are presented in Table. 3. It is evident that the velocity distributions of the dif- fuse component consistently deviate from a standard Gaussian, with the radial component showing the largest departure. This is due to the interplay between mergers and the host halo. As mergers are accreted, the asso- 15 4 2 0 2 4 vr/vr 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(vr) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 Old Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c 4 2 0 2 4 vr/vr 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(vr) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 Young Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 10. Velocity distributions of untraceable mergers, separated into old and young populations. Old untraceable mergers exhibit velocity components that are approximately Gaussian, with the exception of m12w, which",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_027,27,"[km/s]1 Young Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 10. Velocity distributions of untraceable mergers, separated into old and young populations. Old untraceable mergers exhibit velocity components that are approximately Gaussian, with the exception of m12w, which shows noticeable co-rotation. In contrast, younger untraceable mergers deviate significantly from Gaussian due to incomplete dynamical relaxation. These distributions are noisy and irregular due to limited particle statistics, making precise modeling challenging. As they contribute less than 10% of the total local DM mass (see Table S2) , we approximate them as part of the diffuse component and treat any discrepancies as systematic uncertainties. ciated time-dependent fluctuations in the host gravita- tional potential drive violent relaxation and redistribute the energies and orbital structure of the pre-existing dark matter M. Sparre & S. H. Hansen (2012); G. Ogiya et al. (2016); N. W. Evans et al. (2019). Such episodes preferentially affect the radial degree of freedom, broad- ening and reshaping the vrdistribution relative to the equilibrium Gaussian expectation and thereby enhanc- ing the non-Gaussian features observed in the diffuse component. We",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_028,28,"N. W. Evans et al. (2019). Such episodes preferentially affect the radial degree of freedom, broad- ening and reshaping the vrdistribution relative to the equilibrium Gaussian expectation and thereby enhanc- ing the non-Gaussian features observed in the diffuse component. We also find that the best-fit scale param- eters α— for both the Gaussian and generalized Gaus- sian models — are slightly larger than the local circu-lar velocity vc, indicating broader velocity distributions than expected under the SHM. In the Gaussian case, this implies that the inferred velocity dispersion σ=α/√ 2 exceeds the SHM-predicted value of vc/√ 2, further re- inforcing that the diffuse component exhibits modestly enhanced kinematic dispersion compared to the SHM baseline.",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,149,114,False,True,840,954,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_029,29,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we present a framework for recon- structing the local DM velocity distribution based on the halo’s accretion history. As outlined above, we decom- 16 Fiducial Low Resolution High Resolution β α /vc β α /vc β α /vc r2.74±0.31 1.17 ±0.11 2.51 ±0.14 1.30 ±0.06 2.55 ±0.03 1.15 ±0.01 θ2.37±0.20 1.05 ±0.07 2.40 ±0.16 1.10 ±0.06 2.55 ±0.03 1.01 ±0.01 ϕ2.35±0.22 1.08 ±0.09 2.36 ±0.27 1.23 ±0.08 2.16 ±0.05 1.12 ±0.01 Table 3. Best-fit generalized Gaussian parameters for the diffuse DM velocity distribution. The location parameter is consistent with µ≃0 for all components, so we report the remaining shape parameters (e.g., αandβ) for each velocity component. The same fitting is also applied to low- and high-resolution simulations. 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 Redshift0.20.40.60.81.0Cumulative Diffuse Fractionm12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 11. Cumulative growth of the diffuse DM com- ponent as a function of accretion redshift, defined by the methods in Sec. 2.2. The diffuse component can contribute between 40%",5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION,5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_030,30,"fraction of DM contributed by each merger to the solar neighborhood. Observationally, it is extremely challenging to directly determine how much DM each progenitor deposits lo- cally. However, since DM and stars are accreted to- gether—though with different spatial distributions and disruption timescales—it is plausible that the DM mass loss fraction relative to the progenitor is correlated with the corresponding stellar mass loss fraction. Moreover, the total stellar mass of each progenitor and its stellar debris are more accessible observationally, making the stellar mass loss fraction a potentially useful proxy for estimating the deposited DM. To investigate this, we compute the ratio of the DM loss fraction to the stellar loss fraction for each merger (Fig. 14). The ratio rises with increasing redshift before turning over around z∼2, reflecting the different strip- ping histories of the two components. Because stars are 17 4 2 0 2 4 vr/vr 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(vr) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 4 2 0 2 4 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 Diffuse Component Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 12. Normalized velocity distributions",5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION,5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_031,31,"for the corresponding component. GSE debris today is observed to have a highly radial velocity distribution, with β∼0.8–0.9, indicative of an eccentric, plunging orbit (V. Belokurov et al. 2018; J. M. M. Lane et al. 2021). The velocity anisotropies at z= 0 of individual mergers in our sample are shown in Figure S1. Based on these properties, we define “GSE-like” mergers in our simulations as those that satisfy: 1. Moderate-to-high mass: M > 5×1010M⊙ 2. Radial orbit: β >0.6 3. Intermediate accretion time: 0 .5< z < 1.5 0 2 4 Redshift0.00.20.40.60.81.0 10.0010.2510.5010.7511.0011.2511.50log(M/M) m12i m12fm12w m12mm12b m12c Figure S1. Velocity anisotropy parameter βatz= 0 as a function of accretion redshift for individual mergers across all m12 galaxies. Each point represents a merger event, with βcomputed from the present-day kinematics of its associated DM particles.",A.GSE-LIKE MERGERS,A.GSE-LIKE MERGERS,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,175,134,False,True,140,274,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_032,32,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY In this appendix, we discuss limitations in the reconstruction of merger histories and the steps taken to mitigate them. The limitations include misassociation originated from flyby events and subhalo-subhalo mergers that potentially would be misidentified as separate merger events. In the following subsections, we discuss each effect and the consistency checks used to identify and mitigate it. ii Table S1. Representative mergers from m12i and m12f illustrating the dependence of flyby contamination on subhalo peak mass. The contamination rate is defined as the fraction of particles associated with a merger that are located within the host halo at a snapshot preceding accretion. Higher-mass subhalos exhibit larger flyby contamination fractions. m12i m12f Mpeak[M⊙] Contamination (%) Mpeak[M⊙] Contamination (%) Merger I 6.45×10103.2 1 .53×10114.1 Merger II 3.55×10100.3 8 .20×10100.4 0 2 4 6 Redshift103 102 101 100Cumulative Total FractionMpeak>109M Diffuse Standardm12i 3×Rsubhalo Figure S2. Accretion history of the DM component for the m12i subhalo test, in which the radii of all subhalos (excluding the main",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_033,33,"II 3.55×10100.3 8 .20×10100.4 0 2 4 6 Redshift103 102 101 100Cumulative Total FractionMpeak>109M Diffuse Standardm12i 3×Rsubhalo Figure S2. Accretion history of the DM component for the m12i subhalo test, in which the radii of all subhalos (excluding the main halo) were increased by a factor of three, in comparison to it unchanged. The pronounced increase in the diffuse component at z∼4 for both demonstrates that the diffuse buildup during major merger events persists even under enlarged subhalo radii. This behavior reflects the rapid growth of the main halo during these mergers. B.1. Flyby rate One such limitation arises from the flyby rate, in which DM particles that are not genuinely associated with an accreting subhalo are nevertheless identified as part of a merger by the algorithm. This effect is negligible for star particles, which are preferentially concentrated near the centers of subhalos and therefore less susceptible to misclas- sification. In this work, we define a flyby particle as a particle already within the host halo radius prior to formal accretion of the subhalo. The original convention was to trace",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_034,34,"centers of subhalos and therefore less susceptible to misclas- sification. In this work, we define a flyby particle as a particle already within the host halo radius prior to formal accretion of the subhalo. The original convention was to trace particles backward in time, requiring association in three of the last four snapshots, with a velocity dispersion cut of 3 σ. We found that the flyby rate is dependent on the mass of the oncoming subhalo. Larger subhalos attracted a greater number of flybys from the main halo than subhalos with smaller mass. Table S1 displays representative mergers from m12i andm12f and their corresponding contamination rates. To reduce contamination from flyby DM particles, we imposed a stricter accretion criterion. Specifically, a DM particle was required to be associated with a subhalo in at least six of the last nine snapshots, rather than three of the last four. This modification significantly reduced the number of DM particles misassigned while preserving those truly associated with the merger. Nevertheless, a residual population of flyby particles that were never physically bound to the merger",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_035,35,"rather than three of the last four. This modification significantly reduced the number of DM particles misassigned while preserving those truly associated with the merger. Nevertheless, a residual population of flyby particles that were never physically bound to the merger persisted. To test the robustness of the merger reconstruction, we performed several consistency checks. First, we increased the radii of all subhalos (excluding the main halo) by factors of 2, 3, and 4 for m12i andm12f. In each case, the number of particles classified as diffuse decreased, as expected. However, pronounced increases in the diffuse component persisted whenever a massive halo was accreted. We attribute these features to the rapid growth of the main halo’s radius during such events, leading the main halo to expand and include nearby DM particles that are not physically bound to the merger. iii 400 200 0 200 x [kpc]200 100 0100200300y [kpc] |v|3.0subhalo z=0.42Dark matter Stars Rhalo=223.6kpc 400 200 0 200 x [kpc]|v|2.5subhalo z=0.42 Figure S3. Spatial distribution of merger-associated DM (blue) and stellar (red) particles for the same accretion event in the m12f",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_036,36,"factor of three. As in Fig. 2, the diffuse component exhibits sharp increases at major merger events, reflecting the expansion of the main halo during these periods. As a cross-check, we also examined the effect of relaxing the dispersion cut from 2 .5σto 3σ. As anticipated, this change resulted in a higher flyby rate, confirming that looser kinematic cuts lead to increased contamination from non-merging material. Fig. S3 demonstrates the increase in contamination rate as we increase σfrom 2.5 to 3 for Merger 1 of m12f. With σ= 2.5, 4.1% of the particles associated with Merger 1 are misassociated with that merger. By contrast, this value is 7.6% for σ= 3. Conversely, adopting a stricter criterion reduces flyby contamination but may exclude a small number of genuinely associated merger particles. However, we find that this trade-off is strongly asymmetric: the number of flyby particles introduced when increasing to 3 σsubstantially exceeds the number of genuine merger particles lost when tightening to 2.5σ. For Merger 1 of m12f, decreasing the cut from 3 σto 2.5 σremoves roughly 600 flyby particles while",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,700,880,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_037,37,"number of flyby particles introduced when increasing to 3 σsubstantially exceeds the number of genuine merger particles lost when tightening to 2.5σ. For Merger 1 of m12f, decreasing the cut from 3 σto 2.5 σremoves roughly 600 flyby particles while excluding only about 30 genuinely associated particles. This demonstrates that the 2.5 σthreshold effectively suppresses contamination while minimally impacting the true merger signal. Based on the quantitative comparisons above, the final adopted convention for this work is the following: six of nine snapshot requirement, 2.5 σdispersion cut, and default subhalo radii. B.2. Subhalo-subhalo mergers In several cases, mergers identified by the algorithm were required to be combined. This arises because the merger- matching procedure is based primarily on star particle membership. In some situations, a subhalo contained star particles but no associated dark matter particles, leading to the artificial separation of what was physically a single merger event. One example of this behavior occurs for Mergers 2 and 3 in m12f. Merger 2 contained both stellar and DM components, whereas Merger 3 contained a substantial number of star particles but",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_038,38,"of what was physically a single merger event. One example of this behavior occurs for Mergers 2 and 3 in m12f. Merger 2 contained both stellar and DM components, whereas Merger 3 contained a substantial number of star particles but no DM particles. Further inspection revealed that the particles associated with both mergers followed the same orbital trajectory, indicating that they originated from the same accreting system. Consequently, the particles from Mergers 2 and 3 were combined into a single merger, labeled Merger 2. All subsequent merger indices were then shifted accordingly (e.g., Merger 4 →Merger 3). Figure S4 displays the trajectories of the two subhalos up to redshift 3, showing how they are from the same system. The following mergers were combined in this manner: iv 600 500 400 300 200 100 0 x [kpc]0100200y [kpc] Merger 2 Merger 3 Figure S4. Mean stellar orbital trajectories in the x–yplane for Mergers 2 and 3 in m12f, traced backward from z= 0 to z= 3 with positions relative to the main halo. The point marks the present-day ( z= 0)",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_039,39,"3 Figure S4. Mean stellar orbital trajectories in the x–yplane for Mergers 2 and 3 in m12f, traced backward from z= 0 to z= 3 with positions relative to the main halo. The point marks the present-day ( z= 0) position of each stellar component. Although initially identified as separate mergers, the stellar particles associated with both mergers follow nearly identical orbital paths at all times, indicating that they originate from the same accreting system. •m12f: Mergers 2 and 3 •m12m: Mergers 2 and 5 •m12i-lowres : Mergers 3 and 5 In other cases, a merger identified via stellar particles had no DM particles assigned to it through our post-processing phase of assigning DM particles (for more details, see L. Necib et al. (2019)). Upon further inspection, we found that the DM from the same physical progenitor had been assigned to a separate dark subhalo (one lacking stars). By matching the orbital trajectories of these dark subhalos to the stellar mergers, we were able to reunite the two components. The following mergers were connected to dark subhalos in this way:",B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,B.LIMITATIONS ON MERGER HISTORY,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300,False,2.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_000,0,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure present in the centre of many disk galaxies, while a key morphological feature for galaxy classifica- tion, is similarly significant for driving galaxy evolution. Bars have beendemonstratedtoaffecthost-galaxyprocessesonallscales:from star formation on molecular-cloud scales (10−100pc; e.g. Kuno et al. 2000; Dobbs et al. 2006, 2014; Nilipour et al. 2024; Maeda et al. 2025) to galaxy-wide dynamics (∼1000pc; e.g. Regan et al. 1999; O’Neill & Dubinski 2003; Emsellem et al. 2006; Iles et al. 2024;Géronetal.2024;Rutherfordetal.2025);includingdisk–halo and disk–bulge co-evolution (e.g. Athanassoula 2002; Valenzuela & Klypin 2003; Kormendy & Kennicutt 2004; Jogee et al. 2005; Athanassoula et al. 2013; Kruk et al. 2018), angular momentum distribution (e.g. Weinberg 1985; Athanassoula 2005; Romeo et al. 2023), Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) (e.g. Shlosman et al. 1989; Alonsoetal.2014;Garlandetal.2023),Inter-StellarMedium(ISM) structure (Friedli & Benz 1993; Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettitt etal.2020;Katsiolietal.2026),gasdepletionandquenching(Mas- ters et al. 2012; Spinoso et al. 2017; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2020; Géron et al. 2021), for example. Accounting for up to∼75% of nearbydiskgalaxies(e.g.Schinnereretal.2002;Aguerrietal.2009; Nair&Abraham2010;Saha&Elmegreen2018),anincreasingnum- ★E-mail: elizabeth.iles@sydney.edu.au)berofgalaxiesathighredshift(e.g.Guoetal.2023;Costantinetal. 2023; Liang et al. 2024; Le Conte",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_001,1,"Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettitt etal.2020;Katsiolietal.2026),gasdepletionandquenching(Mas- ters et al. 2012; Spinoso et al. 2017; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2020; Géron et al. 2021), for example. Accounting for up to∼75% of nearbydiskgalaxies(e.g.Schinnereretal.2002;Aguerrietal.2009; Nair&Abraham2010;Saha&Elmegreen2018),anincreasingnum- ★E-mail: elizabeth.iles@sydney.edu.au)berofgalaxiesathighredshift(e.g.Guoetal.2023;Costantinetal. 2023; Liang et al. 2024; Le Conte et al. 2024, 2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakada et al. 1991; Paczyn- skietal.1994;Zhaoetal.1994;Bland-Hawthorn&Gerhard2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still lack a distinct, universally-applicable method used to define the spatial extent of the “bar” in these galaxies (see, for recent example: Dehnen et al. 2023; Lucey et al. 2023; Petersen et al. 2024; Iles et al. 2025), let alone to identify whether an in- dividual star is a member of the bar or just passing through. For example,Fourieranalysis(e.g.Elmegreen&Elmegreen1985;Ohta et al. 1990; Aguerri et al. 1998; Garcia-Gómez et al. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosst et al. 2025) has long been used to identify bars, particularly in simulations, while observational studies have traditionally relied on isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abraham et al. 1999;Laineetal.2002;Erwin2005;Gadotti&deSouza2006;Mari- nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestre",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_002,2,"al. 1995; Fortson et al. ©2026 The AuthorsarXiv:2603.24952v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2E.J. Iles et al. 2018; Foster et al. 2025) can reduce this variation but can be simi- larlyinefficientandsubjectiveifnotappliedcarefully.Morerecently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn- ing, via classification, regression, and segmentation (e.g. Abraham et al. 2018; Cavanagh & Bekki 2020; Fluke & Jacobs 2020; Ca- vanagh et al. 2022; Huertas-Company & Lanusse 2023; Cavanagh et al. 2024), have become increasingly prevalent. However, since there are so many feasible methods to identify the bar in galaxies, it is necessary to select a ‘best’ method for each study. This presents a fundamental problem: do any of these methods consistently recover the same structure across all barred galaxies? The definition of a bar, or barred region, is fundamental to many studies of host-galaxy properties, such as gas flow or star formation along the bar (e.g. Downes et al. 1996; Sheth et al. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, such as longer bars in more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter bars in late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffected",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_003,3,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 5 CONCLUSIONS Many methods exist for classifying and identifying the bar-like fea- tures in the morphology of many disk-type galaxies. Observations andsimulationseachpresentadifferentsetofchallengesandoppor- tunities for defining what precisely constitutes the bar in the centre of a galaxy and consequently, how this feature may drive, impact and effect the surrounding galactic structure and properties. Unfor- tunately,untilonedistinctandunifiedmethodisdeveloped,whichis universally applicable, we will always struggle to ensure like-with- like comparisons across studies using different methods to define the bar. This, in turn, has lasting implications for our understanding of galaxy formation and evolution in areas influenced by the bar. Here, we demonstrate through a simulated test galaxy (IsoB; Iles et al. 2022) that the conservation of action may be a viable method to determine the spatial parameters (e.g. radial extent𝑅 bar, orien- tation𝜙 bar) often used to describe the bar region in galaxies. It is also possible to use this property to identify stars (or star particles in a simulation) which are more likely to be on bar orbits and thus, identifying members of the bar",5 CONCLUSIONS,5 CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_004,4,"al. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosst et al. 2025) has long been used to identify bars, particularly in simulations, while observational studies have traditionally relied on isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abraham et al. 1999;Laineetal.2002;Erwin2005;Gadotti&deSouza2006;Mari- nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestre et al. 2007; Consolandi 2016; Jiang et al. 2018) or photometric decomposition (e.g. Reese et al. 2007; Gadotti 2008; Durbala et al. 2008, 2009; Weinzirl et al. 2009; Kruk et al. 2018; Fraser-McKelvie et al. 2025). Visual clas- sification (e.g. Nair & Abraham 2010; Hoyle et al. 2011; Masters etal.2011,2012;Melvinetal.2014;Kruketal.2018;Mastersetal. 2021; Géron et al. 2021) remains common, as bars are inherently visual features, but this is increasingly impractical for large surveys and is inherently subject to significant person-to-person variability (e.g. Lahav et al. 1995; Heidl et al. 2013; Qian et al. 2022; Iles et al. 2025). Crowdsourcing (e.g. Lahav et al. 1995; Fortson et al. ©2026 The AuthorsarXiv:2603.24952v1 [astro-ph.GA] 26 Mar 2026 2E.J. Iles et al. 2018; Foster et al. 2025) can reduce this variation but can be simi- larlyinefficientandsubjectiveifnotappliedcarefully.Morerecently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn- ing, via classification,",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_005,5,"along the bar (e.g. Downes et al. 1996; Sheth et al. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, such as longer bars in more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter bars in late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffected by this systematic bias. So, despite our ability to define the bar in manyways,definingthebarinawaythatisdistinct,repeatable,and applicableacrossbarredgalaxiesmuststillbeapriorityforthefield. It is only in this way that we can most effectively approach a unified understandingofbarstructure,galaxyformation,andtheroleofbars in driving galaxy evolution.",1.1 Background,1.1 Background,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,97,74,False,False,560,634,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_006,6,"to describe the bar region in galaxies. It is also possible to use this property to identify stars (or star particles in a simulation) which are more likely to be on bar orbits and thus, identifying members of the bar on a star-by-star basis. By exploiting the condition of axisymmetry necessary for the ac- tion integrals to be considered adiabatic invariant quantities, we use the conservation of action on a short timescale (Δ𝑡=10Myr) for each star (star particle) as a metric for whether a star is likely to be on any quasi-periodic orbit within an axisymetric potential or, instead, located in a strongly non-axisymmetric part of the galac- tic potential (i.e. the bar). Setting a threshold of<10% change in valueofthetotalaction(Δ𝐽 tot)overthetimeperiodtobeconsidered conservedactions,wecandeterminethefractionofstarsthroughout thediskwithconservedornotconservedaction.Byusingafraction of no more than0.5(or50%) of star particles with conserved ac- tions (|Δ𝐽 tot|<10%) in a given spatial bin as a limit for a strongly non-axisymmetricstructure,weareabletorecoverbarclassification parameters, such as the bar length (𝑅 bar) and orientation (𝜙 bar). For thetestsimulation,theactionconservationcalculationwasachieved for every snapshot and the returned values similar in magnitude and variation across the evolution of the disk",5 CONCLUSIONS,5 CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_007,7,"bin as a limit for a strongly non-axisymmetricstructure,weareabletorecoverbarclassification parameters, such as the bar length (𝑅 bar) and orientation (𝜙 bar). For thetestsimulation,theactionconservationcalculationwasachieved for every snapshot and the returned values similar in magnitude and variation across the evolution of the disk to by-eye classifications of the same galaxy. These values are also similar in magnitude to re- sults from an automated, Fourier-based method of bar classification for this galaxy where these are returned, but appears more resilient",5 CONCLUSIONS,5 CONCLUSIONS,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,99,76,False,False,280,356,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_008,8,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.2 Dynamical Actions We propose a different approach to classifying the bar in galaxies, particularly aimed at identifying bars in simulations with high res- olution capturing complex or non-standard systems, such as double bars, nuclear disks, or bars distorted by interactions, with the added capacity to track time evolution on galactic scales. Further, this pro- cess is designed be able to determine, not only the spatial extent of the bar but also, the membership of individual stars to the bar, dis- tinguishing stars on bar-supporting orbits, or orbits affected by the bar, from those merely passing through. This capability will enable theassessmentofimportantbar-drivenprocesses,includingstarfor- mation, radial migration and chemical mixing, on a more precise, star-by-star basis, at least in simulations. Thebasisofthismethodistoexploittheintrinsicpropertiesofthe angle–actionvariables(𝜃 𝑖,𝐽𝑖)indifferentregionsofthegalacticpo- tential. Action-space, described by these angle-action variables, has long been used to probe many properties of the Milky Way through Galacticdynamics(e.g.Lynden-Bell&Kalnajs1972;Kalnajs1977; Arnold 1978; Martinet et al. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein- berg2001;Sellwood2012).Thisisprimarilyduetotheconvenience of the action integrals, as a set of adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit in a",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_009,9,"used to probe many properties of the Milky Way through Galacticdynamics(e.g.Lynden-Bell&Kalnajs1972;Kalnajs1977; Arnold 1978; Martinet et al. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein- berg2001;Sellwood2012).Thisisprimarilyduetotheconvenience of the action integrals, as a set of adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit in a steady, or slowly varying, potential (see Binney & Tremaine 2008, for a more specific detail). Actions have been instrumental in modelling phase-mixed populations (e.g. Tremaine 1999; Antoja et al. 2018; Ting & Rix 2019; Monari et al. 2019;Drimmeletal.2023),identifyingstellarstreams(e.g.Helmi& White1999;Eyre&Binney2011;Sanders&Binney2013),andbet- ter constraining the dark matter distribution within the Galaxy (e.g. Machado & Manos 2016; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024). Most importantly, the more abstract action-angle co-ordinates can be easily related to ordinary phase-space variables(𝑥,𝑣)(e.g. Bin-ney & Tremaine 2008; Binney 2020), either directly from the stellar orbits or via the instantaneous position and velocity measurements withanapproximationofalocalStäckelpotential(e.g.Binney2012; Sanders & Binney 2015), and algorithms that compute actions are readily available, relatively widespread and mostly computationally inexpensive,practicalevenformillion-particle𝑁−bodysimulations orextremelylargeobservationaldatasets(e.g.Debattistaetal.2020; Mikkola et al. 2020; Zozulia et al. 2024). The most useful invariant property of the action, however, relies primarily on the assumption that the",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_010,10,"2015), and algorithms that compute actions are readily available, relatively widespread and mostly computationally inexpensive,practicalevenformillion-particle𝑁−bodysimulations orextremelylargeobservationaldatasets(e.g.Debattistaetal.2020; Mikkola et al. 2020; Zozulia et al. 2024). The most useful invariant property of the action, however, relies primarily on the assumption that the potential is axisymmetric and invariant. Actions cease to be conserved in non-axisymmetric envi- ronments,suchasbarsandspiralarms(e.g.Binney&Tremaine2008; Solway et al. 2012; Vera-Ciro & D’Onghia 2016); resonances, such asco-rotation(CR;e.g.Sellwood&Binney2002;Roškaretal.2012; Daniel & Wyse 2015) and the inner- and outer-Lindblad resonances (ILR/OLR; e.g. Barbanis & Woltjer 1967; Lynden-Bell & Kalnajs 1972; Carlberg & Sellwood 1985), can significantly alter individual actions; vertical action may be conserved during radial migration in general, but not necessarily on a star-by-star basis (e.g. Solway etal.2012;Vera-Ciro&D’Onghia2016;Mikkolaetal.2020);spiral perturbations can change azimuthal action while leaving radial ac- tion nearly unchanged (e.g. Sellwood & Binney 2002; Roškar et al. 2012); and rapid potential evolution, such as during bar formation andbuckling(e.g.Toomre1981;Sellwood1981;Thielheim&Wolff 1982; Raha et al. 1991), can violate adiabatic invariance entirely. Assumptionsandapproximationshavesoughttomitigatetheeffects of this variance to probe galactic history (McMillan 2011; Binney 2012;Sanders&Binney2015;Antojaetal.2018;Ting&Rix2019; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024), while recent studies have startedtoexplorehowresonancesandtheactiondistributionofstars",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_011,11,"as during bar formation andbuckling(e.g.Toomre1981;Sellwood1981;Thielheim&Wolff 1982; Raha et al. 1991), can violate adiabatic invariance entirely. Assumptionsandapproximationshavesoughttomitigatetheeffects of this variance to probe galactic history (McMillan 2011; Binney 2012;Sanders&Binney2015;Antojaetal.2018;Ting&Rix2019; Malhan et al. 2022; Carlberg et al. 2024), while recent studies have startedtoexplorehowresonancesandtheactiondistributionofstars are related to bars, spirals, and boxy/peanut bulges (e.g. Debattista etal.2020;Kawataetal.2021;Tricketal.2021;Trick2022;Drim- mel et al. 2023; Zozulia et al. 2024; Arunima et al. 2025). In this work, we are only concerned with exploiting the existence of these changes to the action, rather than reducing or categorising their ef- fects.Thepremisehereis:ifactionsaresystematicallynotconserved for bar-located stars but remain broadly conserved elsewhere in the disc,thenthenon-conservationitselfbecomesadiagnostic.Thiswill allowustodirectlyinferbarmembershipandidentifybarproperties through a simple assessment of the conservation of action for stars throughout the galactic potential. Inthiswork,wedemonstratethismethodinthecontextofnumer- ical𝑁−body simulations, making the best use of simulated data to tracktime-evolution.Inacompanionpaper(seealsoIlesetal.2026) we demonstrate the application of this method for the Milky Way bar,usingobservationsofstarsinlargesurveys.Adescriptionofthe method and test simulation is included in Section 2. The definition of a standard bar region (e.g. radial bar extent and pitch-angle) is includedinSection3,whileSection4outlinesthe‘star-by-star’use- case, identifying potential bar membership for each stellar particle inthesimulation.Finally,ourconclusionsandrecommendationsfor future applications of this method are included",1.2 Dynamical Actions,1.2 Dynamical Actions,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_012,12,"mass fractions (see Iles et al. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved for a relatively short period of1Gyr, however, this is sufficient to cover a period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, and a short pe- riod post-bar formation to capture action properties in each of these regimes.LatersnapshotsoftheIsoBdiskcanbeconsideredaclassic example of a barred galaxy, however, automated bar identification methodsdonotconsistentlyproducerealisticbarparametersforthis disk in every snapshot. Thus, we consider this simulation a good elementary demonstration of the proposed method, as there are suf- ficient snapshots with automated bar classification for comparison but automation can struggle with this galaxy. Additionally, we have recentlycompletedvisualclassificationforeachsnapshotoftheIsoB simulation for a study into visual bar classification (Iles et al. 2025) which, despite some limitations, can also be used as a comparison for the bar parameters of this disk as measured ‘by-eye’.","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,173,133,True,False,140,273,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_013,13,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) 4E.J. Iles et al. Figure 2.Stellar mass density (left) and average change in action (Δ𝐽 tot%) for the1Gyr snapshot in edge-on (𝑥𝑧-upper) and face-on (𝑥𝑦-lower) orientation. The projected values are measured along the line-of-sight with a pixel grid of size:𝑥=𝑦=50pc and𝑧=10pc and within a total extent of𝑅 <10kpc, |𝑧|<3kpc. Black contours in the right panel trace stellar density peaks from the left panel. used theagamapackage (Vasiliev 2019) as an alternative method todeterminetheactions.Fortheagamapotential,weinitiallymod- elledthestarsandcoldgas(log𝑇 <4.5)withanazimuthalharmonic expansionandthedarkmatterandhotgaswithasphericalharmonic expansion. Then, to recover the actions in the non-axisymmetric regime,agamaalso makes use of the Stäckel approximation. How- ever, we found that there were a selection of snapshots for which the calculation of actions would fail in these potentials, returning no values. As such, we trialled a different method of construction, where both baryons (stars + gas) and dark matter were instead mod- elled with spherical harmonic expansions. This approach allowed us to determine actions in a wider range of snapshots, with only a slight drop in accuracy (<1%) as compared to the initial","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_014,14,"(stars + gas) and dark matter were instead mod- elled with spherical harmonic expansions. This approach allowed us to determine actions in a wider range of snapshots, with only a slight drop in accuracy (<1%) as compared to the initial method withagama. Although bothgalpyandagamarely on the Stäckel approximation to navigate the non-axisymmetric conditions, there are slight differences in the calculation method and, we find, in the result(seeFigure5inSection3).Finally,forcompleteness,wetested the simplest option. Instead of integrating or modelling the existing potential from the𝑁-body particles, we used standard forms of a MilkyWay-likepotentialandonceagainrecoveredtheactionsusing theagamapackage. In this instance, we used a Dehnen (1993) type bulge,aMiyamoto&Nagai(1975)potentialforthestellardisk,and a NFW (Navarro et al. 1997) potential for the gas and dark matter.Each of these potentials were scaled using the initial scale radii and massesforeachcomponentfromIlesetal.(2022),toavoidextracal- culationsateachsnapshot,asthisisalreadyanapproximation.Wedo not expect this to be the most accurate representation of the galactic potential,however,itisverymuchthesimplest.Incomparingthebar parameters we determine using the actions under each potential, we indeed find some differences but the magnitude of these differences canbeconsideredrelativelyinsignificant.Weincludeadiscussionof thiscomparisonbetweenpotentialsalongsideourresultsinSection3.","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,208,160,True,False,140,300,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_015,15,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 3 THE “BAR REGION” IN PROJECTION As a simple representation of how action change per particle cor- relates with non-axisymmetric structures, Figure2 includes the pro- jectedstellarmassdensity(left)forthe1Gyrsnapshotoftheisolated (IsoB)simulation,alongsidethemeanchangeintotalaction( Δ𝐽tot%, right) calculated along the line-of-sight in grid-square bins (pixels). This is to represent the galaxy as if viewed by an external observer, however,wenotethattheusingthefullextentineachdirectionrather than a pixel-width slice could affect the prominence of the structure seen in the image. The resolution of these pixels are each50pc in 𝑥and𝑦and10pc in𝑧to account for the smaller range in galactic",3 THE “BAR REGION” IN PROJECTION,3 THE “BAR REGION” IN PROJECTION,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,120,92,False,False,0,92,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_016,16,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) 6E.J. Iles et al. Figure4.Ameasureofthebarextent(𝑅 bar)whichisthelimitwherelessthan50%ofstarparticlesinagivenradialbinhaveconstanttotalaction(𝐽 totchanging by 10% or less) as calculated by this work (using method A:galpywith simulation-formed stars within𝑅=10kpc) in purple line, compared with a Fourier bar-finding method (Dehnen et al. 2023) in green dashed-line and by-eye (Iles et al. 2025) in blue dot-dashed-line for the isolated (IsoB) simulation. The bar chart in the lower panel is a representation of the percentage difference between the method employed in this work and the other methods. Grey bars indicate periods where no bar was identified in either of the methods compared. mate which acts as a buffer for absorbing small systematic errors in theactioncalculationandsimulationprocess,whilepreservingmost of the true physical variability. We find that choosing a smaller per- centage changes the exact fractions of stars with conserved actions, but not the overall behaviour, making this a reasonable threshold. To determine a measure of the bar extent, we separate the star particlesintobinsof50pcinradius,whichisequivalenttothestellar softening length in this simulated disk. Similarly, we also separate the star particles into bins of0.0209radians (1.2◦) in","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180,False,3.0
