are resolved and change can be measured directly from snapshot-to-snapshottimeevolution.However,aswedemonstratein (Iles et al. 2026) this concept can also be exploited in observations","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_024,24,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) 10E.J. Iles et al. to complex central structures (e.g. NSD formation) and bar-arm de- coupling,aswellasnoisysystems,suchasgas-richstellardiscswith a highly turbulent ISM (Bland-Hawthorn et al. 2024, 2025; Zhang et al. 2025). As there is currently no available ‘true’ solution to test against the actual bar parameters, we propose that this method ap- pears to be a robust and viable means to identify the bar in galaxies where the action is calculable for a large percentage of stars (star particles) and where it is possible to measure the change in action over time (Δ𝑡≤100Myr). In addition to standard bar region classification, the change in action (Δ𝐽 tot%) can also be used on a star-by-star basis to assess the bar membership of stars from an orbital perspective, as action is fundamentally related to orbit dynamics. Star particles with low changesintotalaction(|Δ𝐽 tot|∼0%)arefoundtobemainlylocated atlargerdiskradiiandonpredominantlycircularorbits.Starparticles with moderate changes in total action (|Δ𝐽 tot|∼50%) are found to be mostly located at inner radii, within co-rotation and the spatially determinedbarextent,onmorecomplexorbits(e.g.inbarorbitfam- ilies). Finally, star particles with the largest changes","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_025,25,"orbit dynamics. Star particles with low changesintotalaction(|Δ𝐽 tot|∼0%)arefoundtobemainlylocated atlargerdiskradiiandonpredominantlycircularorbits.Starparticles with moderate changes in total action (|Δ𝐽 tot|∼50%) are found to be mostly located at inner radii, within co-rotation and the spatially determinedbarextent,onmorecomplexorbits(e.g.inbarorbitfam- ilies). Finally, star particles with the largest changes in total action (|Δ𝐽 tot|≳100%) are almost all centrally located (𝑅 <1kpc) with primarilychaoticorbits.Inthisway,thechangeinactionmayalsoal- lowforadistinctionbetweenstarsinthebarregionbutnotfollowing bar-like orbits (passing through) compared to the true members of thebar(onbarorbits).Thishasthepotentialtoimpactongoingstud- ies of bar-related galactic properties, such as star formation, stellar migration and chemical mixing. Werecommendtheconservationofactionasanalternative,repeat- able, method for classifying bars, at least in simulations of galaxies. Through this work we have sought to demonstrate the following. •It is possible to use the conservation of action to classify the spatial extent of barred region in simulated galaxies. •The percentage change in action can be a good indicator for stellar orbits that contribute to different morphological features in simulated galaxies. While this method, too, has both limitations and advantages, we hopethatthisworkservestoadvanceongoingeffortswithinthefield to unify the classification of bars in galaxies and supports future research into bars, the galaxies that host them, and the evolution of structure in","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_026,26,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicality of the proposed method using 𝑁−body SPH (smoothed particle hydrodynamics) in the form of an isolated disk galaxy simulation (IsoB) from Iles et al. (2022). To test bar-finding on a star-by-star basis, simulations with high tem- poral and spatial resolution were necessary to be able to trace bar",2.1 The Test Simulations,2.1 The Test Simulations,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,85,65,False,False,0,65,False,2.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_027,27,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026) Action Bar Classification3 evolutionandstellarorbits.TheIsoBsimulationhasastellarparticle mass resolution of1044M ⊙for simulation-formed stars, a stellar softening length of 50pc and a snapshot for every 10Myr of galaxy evolution, which is approximately equivalent to the star formation timescale (𝑡 SF=10Myr). This disk was evolved withgasoline2 (Wadsley et al. 2017), comprising a dark matter component, a small stellarbulge,aninitialstellardiskandagasdiskcomponentofparti- clesrespectively,followingthestandardsub-gridprescriptions(Katz et al. 1996; Stinson et al. 2006); with super-bubble feedback (Keller et al. 2014), UV and photoelectric heating, as well as metal cooling in the form of a tabulated cooling function (Shen et al. 2010), re- covering the two-phase ISM (Wolfire et al. 2003) from an initially isothermal (104K) gas profile. Star formation was prescribed by a standard temperature (300K) and density (100atoms/cc) threshold, aswellasaconvergentflowrequirementwithanefficiencyof∼10% andaChabrier(2003)initialmassfunction(IMF).Theinitialcondi- tionwasoriginallytailoredtoobservationsofnearby,resolvedgalaxy ngc4303via the rotation curve, gas and stellar mass fractions (see Iles et al. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved for a relatively short period of1Gyr, however, this is sufficient to cover a period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, and a short pe- riod post-bar","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)","MNRAS000, 1–12 (2026)",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180,False,2.0
