astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_023,23,"the expectedT fitfrom pure phase mixing theory. This can also be interpreted as the difference between the derived perturbation time fromT fitand the true disk crossing time. Right:The 50 Myr moving average of the medianT fitvalue for eachJ ϕat each N-body simulation timestep. In our median calculations, we only include regions withT fitvalues between 0 and 2 times the value expected from phase mixing theory. We see clearly here that while there are winding delays at allJ ϕ, the phase spirals in the inner disk are far more affected by n-body interactions. Note: the conversion fromJ ϕtoR Gin the colorbar is done according to the N-body simulation. The test particle simulation does not have exactly the same scaling (see the two x-axes in Fig. 4 for a visualization of the test-particle scaling). The ratioν/Ω (whereν= Ω z(z= 0) and Ω = Ω ϕ(z= 0)) provides a useful assessment of the dominance of the disk component in a galactic potential. It is identically unity in perfectly spherical systems, and increases with the oblateness of the system. The left panel of Fig.",IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,False,2.5
