astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_028,28,"using the pytreegravpackage (Grudi´ c & Gurvich 2021) for de- termining gravitational potentials and accelerations of N-body simulations. The scale of the local acceleration perpendicular to thez= 0 plane is estimated by 2πGΣ 0, appealing to the acceleration due to an infinitely thin disk where Σ 0is the surface density. We calculate the surface density of stars enclosed within±zof the planefor an annulus at radiusRand width ∆Rand adopt: az,local (R, z) =G",IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,IN THE N-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,94,72,False,True,700,772,False,1.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_000,0,"Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc −1.0×108M⊙ (Launhardt et al. 2002; Nishiyama et al. 2013; Schödel et al. 2014; Gallego-Cano et al. 2020; Sormani et al. 2022). Whether the MW’s NSD is purely axisymmetric remains an open question. Indeed, while a fair fraction of the current liter- ature has treated the NSD as an axisymmetric structure (e.g., Nogueras-Lara 2022), some observational studies have previ- ously suggested the existence of a nuclear bar in the MW: Alard (2001) used H- and K-band star counts from 2MASS to derive the projected stellar surface density in the bulge region. They reported a positive/negative longitude asymmetry which they in- terpreted as a signature of a nuclear bar. Rodriguez-Fernandez & Combes (2008) derived the mass distribution in the Galac- tic Centre by fitting the 2MASS star count map to a model in- cluding a disk, bulge and nuclear bar. Using this inferred grav- itational potential, they performed hydrodynamical simulationsto model the behaviour of the interstellar gas. The resulting gas flow patterns reproduced a characteristic parallelogram feature observed in the longitude-velocity",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_001,1,"model in- cluding a disk, bulge and nuclear bar. Using this inferred grav- itational potential, they performed hydrodynamical simulationsto model the behaviour of the interstellar gas. The resulting gas flow patterns reproduced a characteristic parallelogram feature observed in the longitude-velocity diagram of molecular spec- tral lines, which they interpreted as evidence of a nuclear bar whose major axis is oriented at an angle ofα≈60◦-75◦relative to the Sun-Galactic Centre line. Nishiyama et al. (2005) used red clump (RC) stars at a latitude ofb= +1◦to trace the main bar orientation as a function of longitude (−10.5◦< ℓ <10.5◦). They reported a change in the inclination angle for the Galactic bar at fields with|ℓ|<4◦(corresponding to a nuclear bar radius R∼570 pc), which they interpreted as the effect of a distinct inner bar structure. Gonzalez et al. (2011) later confirmed this result using RCs from the VISTA Variables in the Via Lactea (VVV) survey to trace the orientation of the large-scale bar at latitudesb=±1◦. While the above results seem compelling, Gerhard & Martinez-Valpuesta (2012) employed N-body simu- lations to show that these observed asymmetries",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_002,2,"from the VISTA Variables in the Via Lactea (VVV) survey to trace the orientation of the large-scale bar at latitudesb=±1◦. While the above results seem compelling, Gerhard & Martinez-Valpuesta (2012) employed N-body simu- lations to show that these observed asymmetries and the change in the slope of the star count profiles can be explained without in- voking a nuclear bar. Instead, they demonstrate that the observed features arise naturally from the intrinsic density distribution of a single, barred, boxy bulge, which transitions from elongated to more axisymmetric within the inner few degrees. This transi- Article number, page 1 of 16arXiv:2603.18738v1 [astro-ph.GA] 19 Mar 2026 A&A proofs:manuscript no. main Fig. 1: The face-on (bottom) and side-on (top) stellar density distribution of the models. Left to right: the axisymmetric NSD, isolated strong NSB, isolated weak NSB, and large-scale bar. The large-scale bar model is oriented at−27◦relative to the Solar-GC line (dotted line). The nuclear bar angle,α, is a free parameter. In all cases, the Solar position is (x,y,z)=(−8.2,0,0.012) kpc. tion causes an apparent flattening of the longitude profiles near |ℓ|≈4◦, similar to",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_003,3,"model is oriented at−27◦relative to the Solar-GC line (dotted line). The nuclear bar angle,α, is a free parameter. In all cases, the Solar position is (x,y,z)=(−8.2,0,0.012) kpc. tion causes an apparent flattening of the longitude profiles near |ℓ|≈4◦, similar to the one observed by Nishiyama et al. (2005) and Gonzalez et al. (2011), mimicking the expected signature of a nuclear bar. A key challenge in determining whether a nuclear bar is present in the MW stems from our edge-on perspective of the Galactic Centre, which is heavily obscured by non-uniformly distributed dust (e.g. Bally et al. 1988; Gonzalez et al. 2012; Schultheis et al. 2014; Nogueras-Lara et al. 2021; Henshaw et al. 2023). Notably, most of the gas/dust (∼3/4) within|ℓ|<3.5◦ resides at positive longitudes (e.g., Bally et al. 1988; Henshaw et al. 2023), which complicates the use of star counts and photo- metric methods by producing an apparent deficit of stars at pos- itive longitudes. This introduces significant biases in the results challenging the reliability of photometric analyses. An alternative approach to detecting a nuclear bar involves the use of",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,420,600,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_004,4,"and photo- metric methods by producing an apparent deficit of stars at pos- itive longitudes. This introduces significant biases in the results challenging the reliability of photometric analyses. An alternative approach to detecting a nuclear bar involves the use of stellar kinematics. Sormani et al. (2022) developed a dynamical equilibrium model fitted to 1D kinematic data from the KMOS NSD survey (Fritz et al. 2021). Although their results indicate that the model fits are consistent with an axisymmetric system, it remains uncertain whether the 1D velocity histograms employed in their analysis are adequate to uncover evidence of non-axisymmetry. Nonetheless, nuclear bars are relatively com- mon features in MW–like galaxies, with roughly 50% of barred galaxies with stellar masses above log(M ⋆/M⊙)>10.5 being double-barred (Erwin 2024). Therefore, the possibility that our Galaxy hosts a nuclear bar warrants careful consideration. Despite the current small sample sizes of existing 6D kine- matic datasets, the anticipated availability of large and high- quality datasets from upcoming surveys such as the KMOS VVVX-GalCen1(Nogueras-Lara et al. in prep), the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey withRoman(Terry et al.",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,560,740,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_005,5,"current small sample sizes of existing 6D kine- matic datasets, the anticipated availability of large and high- quality datasets from upcoming surveys such as the KMOS VVVX-GalCen1(Nogueras-Lara et al. in prep), the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey withRoman(Terry et al. 2023), MOONS (Gonzalez et al. 2020) and JASMINE (Kawata et al. 2024) will enable the dissection of the NSD region in unprece- dented detail, allowing for new insights into the formation and evolution of the Galactic Centre. The goal of this paper is to make testable kinematic predictions that can confirm or rule out the presence of a nuclear bar in the MW with future surveys. 1https://www.eso.org/sci/observing/PublicSurveys/ KMOS-surveys-projects.htmlThis paper is organized as follows: Section 2 describes the models employed in this study. Section 3 and Section 4 present the two main kinematic diagnostics for detecting a nuclear bar2: the vertex deviation and the asymmetry observed in theµ ℓversus Galactic longitude,ℓ, distribution. Section 5 provides a discus- sion of the results and their implications. Lastly, Section 6 briefly outlines prospects for future work. 2. The models We consider three models of",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,700,880,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_006,6,"deviation and the asymmetry observed in theµ ℓversus Galactic longitude,ℓ, distribution. Section 5 provides a discus- sion of the results and their implications. Lastly, Section 6 briefly outlines prospects for future work. 2. The models We consider three models of nuclear structure: – NSD: an axisymmetric nuclear stellar disc (Sect. 2.1) – W-NSB: a weak nuclear bar (Sect. 2.2) – S-NSB: a strong nuclear bar (Sect. 2.2) In addition, we employ a large-scale MW model that includes the outer bar (Sect. 2.3). We construct the full models by em- bedding the nuclear components at the centre of the large-scale MW model, which includes an outer bar but no nuclear structure. Note that for the rest of the paper, when referring to the S-NSB and NSD models, these are always embedded in the large-scale bar. Otherwise, we refer to the ""isolated"" NSD or NSB models. Fig. 1 shows edge-on (top) and face-on (bottom) views of the models. Fig. 2 shows the surface density in the plane of the sky of the large-scale MW model without a nuclear component (left), the surface",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_007,7,"NSD or NSB models. Fig. 1 shows edge-on (top) and face-on (bottom) views of the models. Fig. 2 shows the surface density in the plane of the sky of the large-scale MW model without a nuclear component (left), the surface density of the isolated NSD model (center), and the ratio between the two (right). To create the density panels, we rescale the large-scale bar model (see Sect. 2.3 for details) to achieve an NSD/bar surface density ratio consistent with Sor- mani et al. (2022). The combined models are then constructed by randomly sampling stars from each component (nuclear and large-scale bar), with the relative sampling probability at each position (ℓ,b) determined by the surface density ratio shown in the right panel of Fig. 2. This ensures the correct levels of con- tamination from large-scale bar stars. When sampling from the large-scale bar component, stars are drawn from the original, un- scaled model to preserve the correct kinematic properties. The large-scale MW model is described in more detail in Sect. 2.3. 2We also tested Gauss-Hermite moments as a third diagnostic method,",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_008,8,"large-scale bar component, stars are drawn from the original, un- scaled model to preserve the correct kinematic properties. The large-scale MW model is described in more detail in Sect. 2.3. 2We also tested Gauss-Hermite moments as a third diagnostic method, but found it unsuitable for detecting a nuclear bar (see Appendix E). Article number, page 2 of 16 K. Fiteni et al.: Kinematic Diagnostics for Non-Axisymmetry in the Milky Way’s Nuclear Stellar Disc Fig. 2: The surface density of the large-scale MW model with no nuclear component (left), the isolated NSD (middle), and the ratio between the two (right). The ratio illustrates how prominent the MW’s NSD would be if we were to see it from the Sun (see Fig. 1). Fig. 3: Evolution of the NSD model.Left:the bar strength, measured by the FourierA 2parameter, as a function of time. A2is calculated considering all stars within the radiusR max that encloses 98% of the model’s mass.Right:the evolution of Rmaxwith time. The initially stable NSD starts forming a bar at t≈0.5 Gyr, which reaches maximum strength att≈1.7 Gyr. Black and blue",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1120,1300,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_009,9,"A2is calculated considering all stars within the radiusR max that encloses 98% of the model’s mass.Right:the evolution of Rmaxwith time. The initially stable NSD starts forming a bar at t≈0.5 Gyr, which reaches maximum strength att≈1.7 Gyr. Black and blue dashed lines indicate the times at which the S- NSB and W-NSB models are extracted, respectively. We note that vertical proper motions (µ b) could in principle be used to reduce contamination from large-scale bar stars, as these exhibit systematically higher|µ b|compared to the kinemat- ically cooler NSD (e.g., Shahzamanian et al. 2022; Nogueras- Lara 2022). However, as we demonstrate in Appendix F, apply- ing a conservative cut of|µ b|>5 mas yr−1reduces bar contam- ination by at most∼2%, which has negligible impact on our kinematic diagnostics. Lastly, although the large-scale MW model and the NSD/NSB models are each internally self-consistent, we do not evolve them together under their combined gravitational potential. Therefore, the combined models are not fully self- consistent. However, this has a negligible impact on the results given the very different spatial scales of the models. 2.1. The",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_010,10,"we do not evolve them together under their combined gravitational potential. Therefore, the combined models are not fully self- consistent. However, this has a negligible impact on the results given the very different spatial scales of the models. 2.1. The axisymmetric NSD model We adopt the axisymmetric, self-consistent dynamical model of the NSD from Sormani et al. (2022)3. This model was fitted to 3https://github.com/GalacticDynamics-Oxford/Agama/ blob/master/py/example_mw_nsd.pythe line-of-sight and proper motion kinematic distributions ob- tained by combining the KMOS infrared (K-band) spectroscopic survey of Fritz et al. (2021) with a preliminary version of the VIRAC2 proper motion catalogue (Smith et al. 2025). The final fiducial NSD model has a total mass ofM NSD=9.7×108M⊙, an exponential radial scale length ofR disc=74 pc and a vertical scale length ofH disc=26 pc. 2.2. The strong and weak NSB models To obtain isolated nuclear bar models, we evolve the isolated NSD model of Sormani et al. (2022) within the self-consistent MW model of Binney & Vasiliev (2023)4using the N-body code GADGET-4 (Springel et al. 2021). We use softening lengths of 5, 50, and 25 pc for",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1400,1580,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_011,11,"we evolve the isolated NSD model of Sormani et al. (2022) within the self-consistent MW model of Binney & Vasiliev (2023)4using the N-body code GADGET-4 (Springel et al. 2021). We use softening lengths of 5, 50, and 25 pc for the NSD, dark matter, and main stellar disc components, respectively. We find that the initially axisymmet- ric NSD is mildly unstable, and starts forming a nuclear bar after evolving for 0.6 Gyr. The left panel of Fig. 3, illustrates the evo- lution of theA 2bar amplitude, computed via the usualm=2 Fourier moment (e.g., Li et al. 2024), alongside the radius con- taining 98% of the NSD’s mass (right panel). The output cadence is sufficiently fine that theA 2profile is well-sampled across the full evolution; a running average is applied in Fig. 3 to reduce shot noise between successive outputs. The bar amplitude peaks att=1.7 Gyr, after which it begins to weaken. We extract two snapshots from this simulation to construct our barred NSD models: the strong NSB (S-NSB) att=1.70 Gyr (black dashed line) when the bar is at peak strength,",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,1540,1720,False,3.0
astrobench_cbff59e329,astrobench_cbff59e329_chunk_012,12,"base timestep of∆t=0.5 Myr, which provides sufficient temporal resolution that our results are insensitive to the choice of output cadence. Star formation occurs when gas density ex- ceeds 0.1 amu cm−3and temperature falls below 15,000 K, with a star formation efficiency of 5% per dynamical time and Super- nova feedback via the Keller et al. (2014) prescription. The resulting model develops a bar that reaches a stable con- figuration by∼3.5 Gyr and remains approximately constant thereafter. Att=3 Gyr, the bar has a semi-major radius of Rbar∼4.25 kpc and pattern speed ofΩ∼42 km s−1kpc−1, con- sistent with measurements of the MW (e.g., Wegg et al. 2015; Portail et al. 2017). The model also reproduces the characteris- tic bimodal distribution of red clump stars observed along var- ious lines of sight, mimicking the observed morphology of the MW’s boxy/peanut bulge (Gonzalez et al. 2015). We note that this model does not include a metal-poor spheroidal stellar com- ponent in the bulge; we assess the potential impact of such a population in Appendix A and find it has negligible effect on our",−1.0×108M⊙,−1.0×108M⊙,,,,,Kinematic diagnostics for non-axisymmetry in the Milky Way's nuclear stellar disc,"Karl Fiteni, Xingchen Li, Mattia C. Sormani, Victor P. Debattista, Arianna Vasini, Francisco Nogueras-Lara, Jason L. Sanders, Nathan Deg, Mathias Schultheis, Marco Donati, Zi-Xuan Feng",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-19T10:39:35Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18738v1,2603.18738,galactic_dynamics,233,179,True,True,1960,2139,False,3.0
