astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_033,33,"& Valenti (2016). The estimation of its size varies between 4 and 5 kpc. Therefore, we consider a Rbar= 4.5 kpc which is consistent with the values found in the literature (e.g., Wegg et al. 2015, Tepper-Garcia et al. 2021 and Ghosh et al. 2023), and we also assume a flattening of 0.7, as listed in Table 2. Rotation speed : There are two main scenarios pre- sented in the literature on the value of the bar’s rota- tion pattern speed. The fast rotating bar with Ω bar≳ 50.0 km s−1kpc−1(e.g., Dehnen 2000) is the old one which could explain the origin of HMG by the mecha- nism of the -2:1 LR. The most recent scenario uses the slow rotating bar with Ω bar≲40.0 km s−1kpc−1(e.g., P´ erez-Villegas et al. 2017), which is the most acceptable 6 Fig. 4.— The topology of the Hamiltonian (12) on the X–Y plane, and the co-rotation zones originated by the spiral arms (black curves) and the central bar perturbations (red curves). The stable and unstable centers (equilibrium solutions) are indicated by the dot and",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,840,1020,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_034,34,"components were calculated through Eq.(3), for the spiral arm structure, and Eq.(6) (deducting spherical part), for the central bar, along the line of sight from the Sun to the Galactic center (φ= 90 deg in the reference frame described below). Close to the Sun’s position (vertical dashed line), the forces exerted by the bar and the spiral arms structure are of the same order of magnitude.",TABLE 2,TABLE 2,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,86,66,False,True,1260,1326,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_035,35,"resonances can be observed on both maps, where they are depicted as chains of islands of different widths. The subsets of stars selected from the main MGs in Fig. 2 (left panel) illustrate, by black dots, the positions of the main MGs with respect to the resonances. The corotation zones (labeled as CR) appear on both planes in Fig. 6. The spiral CR (hereafter, SCR; left panel) and its influence zone3incorporate three large MGs, Coma-Berenices, Hyades/Pleiades and Sirius. The Sun, whose position is shown by a star symbol, also be- longs to the SCR zone. It is worth mentioning that these groups are oriented along the boundary of the spiral coro- tation zone that can be related with the large amplitudes of the Lz–variation within the corotation (see Fig. 8 be- low). Similar slopes are significantly smaller for the two Hercules groups, HMG I and HMG II, aligned along a constant Lz-level; their locations are associated with the 12:1 SLR and 8:1 SLR, respectively. The corotation zone of the bar is located in the smaller Lz–values and only its upper",6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_036,36,"on the dynamical maps. The resonant zones analyzed previously on the pR–Lz planes can now be identified as chains of islands extend- ing beyond the immediate vicinity of the Sun. The SCR zone produced by the spiral arms (top panel) is a promi- nent structure near the SNd, along with the inner and outer high-order SLRs, 12:1 and -16:1. Three MGs, such as Sirius, Coma-Berenices and Hyades/Pleiades, are in- volved in the regions of the SCR and its influence zone. The HMG II group, a component of the Hercules mov- ing group, appears as a effect of the 8:1 SLR, while HMG I is more closely aligned with the -6:1 SLR generated by the central bar (see Fig. 7 bottom). The Hyades/Pleiades group is clearly affected by the strong -4:1 BLR of the bar, even if the group’s position matches the location of the unstable center of this resonance. The other MGs cannot be associated with the effect of any BLR. The most prominent bar resonance zones, the BCR and the -2:1 BLR, are beyond the completeness interval of the",6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,980,1160,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_037,37,"the location of the unstable center of this resonance. The other MGs cannot be associated with the effect of any BLR. The most prominent bar resonance zones, the BCR and the -2:1 BLR, are beyond the completeness interval of the observational data, indicating that these resonances cannot explain the dynamical origin of structures close to the SNd.",6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,6.DYNAMICAL VIEW OF THE MOVING GROUPS,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,75,57,False,False,1120,1177,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_038,38,"their turn points on the Poincar´ e map (bottom panel) is plotted by the green and blue chains of islands, respectively. When com- paring the resonant orbits and dynamical structures of the maps for both the spiral arm and bar models, we can observe notable qualitative similarities, with differ- ences mainly in quantitative characteristics. Thus, the main effects of small perturbations that originated from a non-axisymmetric structure on the axisymmetric disk are resonances whose symmetry, positions, and ampli- tudes will depend on the chosen parameters. The vertical component of the angular momentum, Lz, is fundamental in examining the effect of perturbations on the orbits of stars. In fact, in an unperturbed prob- lem,Lzis an integral of stellar motion. In the perturbed, but non-resonant case, the Lz–variations are small that can be observed on the Poincar´ e maps as slight undula- tions of the nearly constant Lz–values (Figs. 8–9). When approaching a resonance, the amplitude of the Lzoscil- lation increases, finally achieving its peak values at the resonant state. The variation of Lzmay be used as a resonance measurement: elongations of",MOVING GROUPS,MOVING GROUPS,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,2.0
astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_039,39,"by the model used to represent the 11 non-axisymmetric components of the Galactic potential. Thus, comparing two planes, we conclude that the res- onance zones of the spirals align more closely with the MGs positions, despite the spiral force function being less intense than that of the central bar in the region of the SNd (see Fig. 3).",8.PARAMETRIC PLANES,8.PARAMETRIC PLANES,,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,76,58,False,False,840,898,False,2.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_000,0,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.MODEL AND SIMULATION In our previous work (Deng et al. 2024), we developed a realistic merger model for the MW. This model successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp, capturing almost all its observed features. However, recent studies (Jiao et al. 2023; Ou et al. 2023) suggest that the MW rotation curve declines more rapidly at larger radii. Therefore, an improved model is needed to accurately represent the mass distribution of the galaxy. The numerical initial conditions (ICs) were generated using the Disk Initial Conditions Environment (DICE) code (Perret et al. 2014; Perret 2016). Density profiles for DM, stars, and gas were input into DICE as distribution functions. These functions were then used to generate Lagrangian particles via the Metropolis-Hastings Monte Carlo Markov Chain algorithm (Metropolis et al. 1953). The mass resolution is 1×105M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolution is sufficient to track the warp structures, as the presence of warp has also been observed in some lower resolution cosmological simulation such as TNG100",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_001,1,"et al. 1953). The mass resolution is 1×105M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolution is sufficient to track the warp structures, as the presence of warp has also been observed in some lower resolution cosmological simulation such as TNG100 (Semczuk et al. 2020). The modeling approach in this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implemented a star formation and feedback module following Wang et al. (2012), enabling gas particles to convert into stellar particles and vice versa through feedback mechanisms. GIZMO is a variant of the smooth particle hydrodynamics code Gadget-3, employing Adaptive Gravity Softening (see details in Hopkins et al. 2018).",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,137,105,True,False,140,245,True,4.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_002,2,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION Due to the high eccentricity, the merger progresses rapidly, ta king approximately 1 Gyr between the first and final pericenter passages and completing within the initial 3 Gyrs. In Figure 1, we present the rotation curve at 11 Gyr 5 102 101 100 r/rvir102103104105106107108DM [M kpc3] 102 101 100 r/rvir0.000.250.500.751.001.251.501.75Mtotal(<R)[M] 1e11 102 101 r/rvir0.00.20.40.60.81.0 101 r/rvir0.00.10.20.30.40.5Triaxiality 101 r/rvir010203040TiltAngle[deg] -200000 -100000 0 E(105km2s2) 100101102103104log(n)0.00.20.40.6 Stellar halo DM halo Figure 2.Top left panelshows the DM density profile along with the radius.Top middle panelshows mass enclosed within a given radius of DM halo. It can be observed that the mass of the dark matter halo in our model is concentrated within 0.2 r vir. Top right panelis the anisotropy parameterβof the stellar halo (blue solid line) and the DM halo (gray solid line) with Galactocentric distance in a spherical coordinate system.Bottom left panelshows the triaxiality of the DM halo, which is measured with DM particles for each bins, as the radius is divided into",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_003,3,"solid line) and the DM halo (gray solid line) with Galactocentric distance in a spherical coordinate system.Bottom left panelshows the triaxiality of the DM halo, which is measured with DM particles for each bins, as the radius is divided into 100 bins with the same number of particles from 0 to r vir, the dense polylines around∼0.1 r virexhibits a highly oblate shape.Bottom middle panelis the tilt angle between DM halo’s short axis with the disk plane in each radius bins, and it shows an increasing trend from the center outward.Bottom right panel shows hist profile of DM halo energy distribution within r vir. for the 15◦inclination case as an example, as differences between various inclination angle settings are negligible. Observational data from Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023) are included for comparison, demonstrating good agreement with our simulation. The rotation curve exhibits no flattening and shows a noticeable decline beyond ∼15 kpc. Since the outer region of the RC mainly depends on the DM profile, additional details of the DM halo are provided in Figure 2.",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_004,4,"our simulation. The rotation curve exhibits no flattening and shows a noticeable decline beyond ∼15 kpc. Since the outer region of the RC mainly depends on the DM profile, additional details of the DM halo are provided in Figure 2. The virial radius (R vir) of this MW model is about 131.05 kpc, defined as the radius within which the average dark matter density equals 200 times the critical density of the universe (ρ cr). This value is slightly higher than the 121.03 and 123.80 kpc reported by Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. The top-middle panel of Figure 2 shows the enclosed mass within the virial radius (R vir) of the DM halo. Including the baryonic mass, the total mass is approximately 2.4×1011M⊙, comparable to the range of 2.44−2.53×1011M⊙and 3.8−5.4×1011M⊙reported by Jiao et al. (2023) and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. Here, we measured the anisotropy parameter (β) for both stellar and DM halos, illustrating its radial variation using different colors. The parameterβis defined as: β= 1−σ2 θ+σ2 ϕ 2σ2r,(2) Here,σ irepresents the velocity dispersions",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_005,5,"and Ou et al. (2023), respectively. Here, we measured the anisotropy parameter (β) for both stellar and DM halos, illustrating its radial variation using different colors. The parameterβis defined as: β= 1−σ2 θ+σ2 ϕ 2σ2r,(2) Here,σ irepresents the velocity dispersions in spherical coordinates. The stellar halo exhibits an increasing anisotropy trend at smaller radii, followed by a subsequent decline, similar to the observed features of GSE debris. In contrast, the anisotropy of the DM halo steadily increases up to the virial radius, indicating the orbits of DM particles become increasingly radial. We also determine the shapes and orientations of the principal axes of the halo using a standard iterative procedure based on the shape tensor method (Emami et al. 2021). In summary, particles are selected within the ellipsoidal volume defined by r min≤rell<rmax, where the ellipsoidal radius r ellis defined as: |rell|2=rbody,1 a2 +rbody,2 b2 +rbody,3 c2 ,(3) 6 and rbody=ET(r−r 0).(4) Here,r body describes particle positions within the halo “body” frame (i.e., its position with respect to the halo principal axes). The halo centerr 0is taken to be the",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,420,600,False,3.0
