astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_010,10,"amplitudes, the stellar and gas disks within the same inclination group also show comparable evolutionary trends in Figure 4. This suggests they are dominated by the same evolutionary mechanism or influenced by the same gravitational potential. In high-inclination groups, the warp amplitude is larger during the early stages, possibly due to the higher vertical angular momentum of the merger orbit. Most notably, a regeneration mechanism appears in the warp evolution. At times, the disk warp amplitude declines to a near-flat state, only to regenerate and rise again to a new peak. Since the simulation involves only a single initial merger event, this persistent, quasi-periodic regeneration provides strong evidence that the warp is driven by long-lived internal processes. 4.2.Kinematic warp model In the previous subsection, we examined the evolution of the Galactic warp, emphasizing that its rapid changes in amplitude reveal the inherently dynamical nature of warps. Understanding this dynamism is essential for tracing the formation history of galaxies and determining the mass distribution of their halos. The changing geometry of the warp can be characterized by the variation in the",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,840,1020,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_011,11,"warp is evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effect on the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model can be expressed in the same form as Equation 7 in Poggio et al. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ) = (Vϕ R−ω)h w(R)cos(ϕ−ϕ w).(10) Using this kinematic model, once the geometric shape is determined by fitting Eq.7, we can substitute the parameters hwandϕto measure the precession rateωbased on the simulation’s kinematic data. For this analysis, we select young stars (age<1.5 Gyr) within the radial rangeR= 7−17 kpc. The region is then divided into 20 radial bins to track and plot the temporal evolution ofω. Figure 5 shows time evolution of stellar disk precession across different inclination groups. Long-term prograde precession is more frequently observed in the high-inclination group, although transient high prograde precession is also occasionally observed in the low-inclination group, it appears as a temporary feature and is generally dominated by retrograde precession for the majority of the simulation time. The disk warp can be described as an m = 1 bending wave propagating in a kinematically cool disk. Such",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1120,1300,False,3.0
