astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_034,34,"it appears as a temporary feature and is generally dominated by retrograde precession for the majority of the simulation time. The disk warp can be described as an m = 1 bending wave propagating in a kinematically cool disk. Such a wave can be viewed as the superposition of ‘fast’ (+) and ‘slow’ (−) waves circulating with frequenciesω=mΩ(R)±ν(R), where Ω(R) is the angular rotation curve,ν(R) is the vertical frequency, and m-fold rotational symmetry is assumed. The inclusion of the disk’s selfgravity raisesν(R), making bending waves more stable, i.e. stiffer (contrary to density waves) – see Binney & Tremaine (2008). For a given rotation curveω(R), bending waves can only propagate in regions that satisfy the condition [ΩP−Ω(R)]2≥ν2 h.(11) where Ω p=ω/mis the pattern speed andν his the frequency of vertical oscillation contributed by the halo potential. This defines, for m = 1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−ν h<Ω p<Ω+ν h, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν >Ω, so the ‘fast’ wave is prograde, with a frequencyωdepending strongly onRfor most radii, so differential rotation winds it up rapidly and it decays. The",4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,4.GALACTIC VERTICAL WAVES,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,1260,1440,False,1.5
